\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Danish bond yields drop as  \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions \highlight\uc2  stoke rate cut talk\b0\par\par\uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2022\par 11:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates with context, detail , chart)\par \par \uc2 By Dhara Ranasinghe\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 5 (Reuters) - Denmark's short-dated borrowing costs fell sharply on Wednesday, as news of heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the Danish crown in recent weeks stoked speculation over a cut in interest rates.\par \par \uc2 According to data released on Tuesday, \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 47 billion Danish crowns, worth more than $7 billion, last month to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg against the euro.\par \par \uc2 While it has been selling crowns to stabilise its strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2021, December marked its biggest monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since early 2020.\par \par \uc2 Economists reckon that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be followed up with a rate cut to dent the crown. The \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sole mandate is to maintain the peg and keep the crown within a band of 7.29252 to 7.62824 crowns per euro.\par \par \uc2 The rate cut speculation is clearly playing out in bond markets where bond yields fell sharply.\par \par \uc2 The yield on Denmark's three-year bond was down almost 7 basis points (bps) on Wednesday at -0.50%.\par \par \uc2 Five-year bond yields were down 3 bps at -0.34%.\par \par \uc2 Rate cuts tend to push down borrowing costs in financial markets and the fall in bond yields suggest investors are starting to position for a move.\par \par \uc2 At -0.60%, the key Danish interest rate is already the among the lowest in the world and a rate cut would contrast with policy in most other developed economies, where central banks are scaling back stimulus and lifting borrowing costs from ultra-low levels as inflation picks up.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates in September after months of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to sufficiently weaken the crown. While money market futures so far suggest only a 20% chance of a 10 bps rate cut in February, speculation is building.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has yet again been forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  markedly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to counter the pressure for a stronger Danish krone," Nordea chief analyst \uc2 Jan St\u248\'00\'F8rup Nielsen\uc2  chief analyst said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "Unless excess liquidity starts to increase more substantially, another interest rate cut could be imminent."\par \par \uc2 Soren Kristensen, chief economist at \uc2 Sydbank\uc2 , agreed a rate cut was likely, seeing a possible 10-15 bps move during the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Dhara Ranasinghe in London and Nikolaj Skydsgaard in Copenhagen Editing by Sujata Rao and Mark Potter) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-1-5T11:06:05.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220105ei1501ard\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
