\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Denmark 'AAA/A-1+' Ratings; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,155 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2019\par 22:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 8 Mar 2019 16:12 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Denmark 'AAA/A-1+' Ratings; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- Denmark's economy is in a strong position, in our view, and will \par continue to grow by 1.7% over the coming years, despite slower global trade \par and a reduction in households' ratio of debt to disposable income. \par      -- Denmark continues to benefit from a strong institutional framework \par that promotes fiscal discipline, and a modest public-sector debt burden. \par      -- We are therefore affirming our 'AAA/A-1+' ratings on Denmark. \par      -- The outlook remains stable. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On March 8, 2019, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its unsolicited 'AAA' long-term \par and 'A-1+' short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on \par Denmark. The outlook is stable. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook reflects our expectation that Denmark's strong economic, \par fiscal, external, and monetary credit metrics can withstand most foreseeable \par shocks. We expect Denmark will maintain low government debt, which we see as \par an important factor to offset some of the risks associated with the country's \par still-high household indebtedness and banks' significant external funding \par needs. \par  \par We could lower the rating if we observe material slippages in fiscal or \par economic performance stemming from weaker institutional and policymaking \par capabilities. The rating could also come under pressure if the stability of \par the Danish financial sector was jeopardized by worsening asset quality in the \par highly indebted private sector, or if external financing comes under pressure. \par  \par RATIONALE \par The rating reflects our view of Denmark's mature political and institutional \par framework, which promotes economic prosperity, competitiveness, and \par flexibility. Longstanding fiscal discipline is also a strength for the rating. \par These factors have supported consistently strong current account surpluses, \par contributing to Denmark's substantial net external asset position. \par Nevertheless, we note potential risks stemming from the financial sector's \par large external borrowing requirements and households' still-high debt, despite \par their deleveraging. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Strong economic growth amid global slowdown \par      -- We expect the Danish economy will expand by an average of 1.7% in the \par next three years, despite households reducing debt and falling world trade \par possibly weighing on growth in the medium to long term. \par      -- We expect the resilience of Denmark's consensus-led politics to \par continue after the 2019 elections. \par      -- Balanced demand fundamentals and macroprudential efforts cushion risks \par of widening budgetary deficits. \par  \par Denmark benefits from a wealthy and diversified economy. Its GDP per capita, \par which we estimate at about $61,600 in 2018, is among the highest of all the \par sovereigns we rate. Furthermore, high-value-added sectors dominate the \par economy, the workforce is skilled and flexible, and the labor participation \par rate is high. \par  \par The low GDP growth rate in 2018 does not reflect an accurate picture of the \par economy's underlying performance, due to patent payments in 2017 inflating \par both exports and GDP. We expect growth to pick up in 2019, during which \par exports should expand and a better growing season for the agriculture sector \par is likely. Over 2020-2021, we think that economic progress will continue, \par supported by strong employment growth, steadily rising disposable incomes, and \par low interest rates. Moreover, we think that increasing financial wealth will \par foster private consumption and economic growth in the coming years. \par  \par We believe the minority government has been successful in implementing \par measures to counteract risks from the tightening labor market, which in turn \par will enhance growth prospects and help ensure the government's long-term \par financial strength. The measures--which include reforms that increased the \par pension age and incentivized the inflow of mainly skilled foreign labor--have \par helped increase the workforce. That said, we believe employment is nearing \par full capacity, which could dampen Denmark's economic growth. \par  \par We note that households have been deleveraging and increasingly moving to \par fixed-rate mortgages over the past few years, thereby reducing their \par sensitivity to interest rate hikes. But even though gross household debt to \par disposable income has reduced from the 2009 peak of 270% to about 215% in late \par 2018, Denmark's ratio of bank credit to the resident private sector is the \par highest among European peers. However, thanks to sizable assets, mainly in the \par form of real estate and pension savings, supported by tax benefits, household \par wealth on a net basis is strong. \par  \par We don't anticipate significant demand-led imbalances, as was the case before \par the property market slump in 2008. Even though property prices have surpassed \par pre-2008 levels, credit growth has stayed modest, suggesting that house prices \par are not fueled by excessive lending. In addition, growth in consumption and \par investment levels has been moderate. This, together with the high savings \par rate, helps prevent potential overheating. Moreover, a change in the property \par tax from 2021 will restore the link between property values and the tax \par levied, which we believe will strengthen the automatic stabilizers in the real \par estate market. In addition, we have seen several initiatives to contain \par household indebtedness over the past years, through lowered deductibility of \par interest payments, rules supporting fixed-rate debt, and amortization for high \par loan-to-value ratios, and thresholds on debt-to-income ratios. These measures \par specifically affect the property markets of Copenhagen and Aarhus, where real \par estate prices tend to be higher. \par  \par Denmark's Liberal-led, minority government must seek coalition partnerships to \par guide legislation through parliament. We note that it gained sufficient \par support to pass its budget for 2019. We also acknowledge the government's \par success in obtaining support for key policies, including the new property tax \par and pension reform. We view Denmark's consensus-led political landscape as \par supportive of the rating, since the current situation presents little risk to \par effective decision-making, despite it being a minority government. We believe \par consensus-based policies will continue after the 2019 election. We consider \par that Denmark's strict budgetary framework prevents excessive spending or \par material structural fiscal deficits. \par  \par Following the publication of the "Report on the Non-Resident Portfolio at \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Estonian branch", which revealed suspicious transactions of \par around EUR200 billion, Danish and European authorities are following up with a \par number of further investigations to examine the effectiveness of cross-border \par financial regulations. Should the investigations reveal any institutional \par weakness or governance issue, we expect effective countermeasures will be \par implemented in a timely manner. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: Substantial current account surpluses \par enhance the external position \par      -- Denmark enjoys very strong current account surpluses on the back of \par solid trade balances and robust investment revenues from overseas assets, \par although at a lower level than in past years. \par      -- Low public debt, despite public housing coming onto the central \par government balance sheet, provides strong fiscal flexibility. \par      -- Predictable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  response patterns continue to uphold the \par krone's peg to the euro. \par  \par Denmark's external performance continues to enhance its net creditor position. \par For more than two decades, Denmark has posted particularly strong current \par account surpluses, peaking at about 9% of GDP in 2014. Stagnating exports in \par 2018 led to a decrease in the current account surplus to 5.5% of GDP, which we \par believe will pick up in 2019 and remain around 6.0%. \par  \par High external savings, as shown by a net external asset position of about 100% \par of current account receipts (CARs) at year-end 2018, continue to generate \par substantial net investment income. The large income surplus supports continued \par strong current account positions. \par  \par Using a narrower measure of Denmark's international investment position, we \par estimate that external debt, net of public- and financial-sector assets, will \par average 47.6% of CARs in 2019-2022, down from about 52% in 2016. We forecast \par current account payments plus short-term debt repayments will remain stable at \par about 184% of CARs plus official reserves in 2019. Roughly three-quarters of \par Denmark's external debt is owed by its financial sector. \par  \par Denmark's monetary policy is, in our view, limited by the peg of the krone to \par the euro, in contrast to policy of sovereigns with floating and more actively \par traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Following the strain on Denmark's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg in the first \par half of 2015, monetary policy has normalized, with the monetary policy rate \par 0.25 percentage points below the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  's (\f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's) policy rate. \par Periods of international uncertainty typically increase investors' appetite \par for the Danish krone, increasing the likelihood of appreciation pressure. In \par early 2019, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Denmark\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   had to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the krone. If \par the krone continues to weaken, we would expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Denmark's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   to \par support it by raising the interest rate on par with that of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par  \par We consider Denmark's fiscal policy track record to be strong. For 2018, we \par expect a headline fiscal surplus of 0.2% of GDP. Because the government is \par seeking to contain expenditures and has reduced taxes, we see potential for \par additional growth-boosting reforms. These include further tax reductions, \par property tax refunds, and repayments of early retirement contributions. These \par reforms spur consumption, creating extra demand, and increase the capacity of \par the economy. \par  \par Denmark has low government debt, thanks to its budgetary performance. As such, \par we expect gross general government debt will remain below 40% of GDP through \par 2022. We note that the central government has decided to finance the social \par housing sector directly by purchasing social housing associations' bonds, and \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 8 Mar 2019 16:13 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Denmark 'AAA/A-1+' -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par by increasing the issuance of government securities. Nevertheless, we project \par net debt to GDP will increase only modestly and remain low, at about 22% of \par GDP at year-end 2022. \par  \par We believe the Danish banking sector overall is stable and represents limited \par contingent liabilities to the sovereign. The banking sector continues to \par record sound profitability on the back of limited loan losses and good \par profitability of mortgage lending, while adhering to strict cost controls. \par Although we have observed a decline in the coverage of nonperforming loans, it \par remains higher than pre-crisis level. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks, Sept. 14, 2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, March 5, 2019 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, March 5, 2019 \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment Update: February 2019, Feb. \par 22, 2019 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, Feb. 4, 2019 \par      -- Eurozone Sovereign Rating Trends 2019, Jan. 14, 2019 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, Dec. 13, 2018. An interactive version is \par also available at " \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2 " \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2017 Annual Sovereign Default Study \par And Rating Transitions, May 8, 2018 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee