\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Denmark At 'AAA/A-1+'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2017\par 22:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 15 Sep 2017 16:11 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Denmark At 'AAA/A-1+'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 : \par  \par Ratings: \par Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : AAA/Stable/A-1+ \par  \par For further details see ratings list. \par  \par OVERVIEW \par  \par      -- We expect Denmark's wealthy, competitive, and diversified economy will \par expand by 2% per year on average during 2017-2020. \par      -- Denmark benefits from high institutional effectiveness, mature \par political and institutional frameworks that promote fiscal discipline, and a \par modest government debt burden. \par      -- We are therefore affirming our 'AAA/A-1+' ratings on Denmark. \par      -- The stable outlook reflects the depth of Denmark's economic, fiscal, \par external, and monetary buffers. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par  \par On Sept. 15, 2017, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its 'AAA' long-term and 'A-1+' \par short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on the Kingdom \par of Denmark. The outlook is stable. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par  \par The stable outlook reflects our expectation that Denmark's strong economic, \par fiscal, external, and monetary credit measures can withstand most foreseeable \par shocks. We expect that Denmark will maintain low government debt, which we see \par as important to offset some of the risks associated with the country's high \par household debt and banks' significant external funding needs. \par  \par We could lower the ratings if policies weakened, leading to slippages in \par fiscal, economic, or external performance. The ratings could also come under \par pressure if high private-sector debt were to jeopardize the stability of \par Denmark's financial sector. We see these scenarios as unlikely to materialize \par over the coming two years, however. \par  \par RATIONALE \par  \par The ratings reflect our view of Denmark's mature political and institutional \par framework--which promotes economic prosperity, competitiveness, and \par flexibility--as well as long-standing fiscal discipline. These factors have \par supported consistently strong current account surpluses, contributing to \par Denmark's substantial net external asset position. We note some potential risks \par stemming from the financial sector's large external borrowing requirements and \par still-high household debt. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Capacity constraints dampen the strong \par growth potential \par      -- High economic growth momentum, but labor market shortages are likely to \par limit medium-term growth potential. \par      -- The consensus-led political landscape will be tested in the upcoming \par 2018 budget vote as the minority government looks to garner support for tax \par cuts and expenditure containment. \par      -- Balanced demand fundamentals and macroprudential efforts cushion risks \par of widening imbalances. \par  \par Denmark benefits from a wealthy and diversified economy. GDP per capita in \par Denmark is among the highest of all the sovereigns we rate, estimated at about \par US$54,800 in 2017. High-value-added sectors dominate the economy, the workforce \par is skilled and flexible, and the labor participation rate is high. At the same \par time, although shrinking, household gross debt to net disposable income stood \par at 292% at year-end 2016, near its 340% peak of 2008. The ratio of bank credit \par to the resident private sector is the highest among countries in the \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2 ). Even so, we note \par that, over the past three years, households have been amortizing debt and \par increasingly moving to fixed-rate mortgage loans, thus reducing their \par sensitivity to interest rate hikes. Moreover, the highest income earners carry \par the largest debt, which mitigates some of the risks. In addition, household net \par wealth is strong, thanks to significant assets primarily in the form of real \par estate and pension savings, which tax-system benefits also encourage. Still, \par most household assets are in the form of illiquid pension account balances and \par real estate. \par  \par The Danish economy is exhibiting strong growth momentum, and we estimate \par economic output will expand by 2.3% in 2017, compared with our previous \par forecast of 1.7%. We see meaningful growth contributions from domestic private \par demand, with sound support from the Danish export industry as it benefits from \par the pickup in demand from key trading partners. In addition, the labor market \par remains solid, with employment up by 2.6% in 2016 and continuing its positive \par trend in 2017, and the unemployment rate set to reduce to 6.0% by 2019. Yet \par despite the healthy labor market, wage increases have been kept in check, \par sustaining Denmark's overall competitiveness. We note high confidence levels \par among households and industries, suggesting that growth will remain sound \par through 2018. \par  \par We believe measures to counteract risks from the tightening labor market could \par help maintain the positive momentum of the business cycle. For instance, recent \par reforms that have raised the early retirement age, alongside an inflow of \par foreign labor, have contributed to expansion of the labor force. Yet we foresee \par labor capacity constraints dampening growth potential in 2018-2020, possibly \par reducing GDP growth to around 2%. \par  \par We consider the current upswing to be solid and well balanced, so we don't \par anticipate mounting demand-led imbalances, as occurred during the years leading \par up to the sharp correction in the Danish property market in 2008. Even though \par we observe a pickup in the real estate market, with house prices surpassing \par pre-2008 levels, credit growth remains modest, suggesting that house prices are \par not being fuelled by excessive lending. In addition, consumption and investment \par ratios, although increasing, are moderate, which together with sound savings \par help prevent overheating. Moreover, we note that a change in the property tax \par from 2021 will restore the link between property value and the tax being \par levied, which we believe will strengthen the automatic stabilizers in the real \par esate market. In addition, macroprudential measures targeting the property \par markets of Copenhagen and Aarhus will likely curb price increases. \par  \par Denmark's minority coalition government needs to seek partnerships to guide \par legislation through parliament. This situation is not unusual in Denmark's \par consensus-led political landscape, suggesting that the current political \par situation presents little risk to effective decision-making. Importantly, we \par observe that political and institutional frameworks, including a strict \par budgetary framework, allow for high institutional effectiveness and government \par discipline, thereby avoiding excessive spending or material structural fiscal \par deficits. Even if elections were held early, which is not our base case, we do \par not expect this would have a material adverse impact on Denmark's institutional \par and policy effectiveness. We observe that the current government has recently \par succeeded in obtaining support for key policies, including the new property tax \par and pension reform, to make it more attractive to stay in the labor market. \par  \par Amid the buoyant economic growth, government has presented a 2018 budget bill \par focused on tax cuts and expenditure containment in efforts to extend the \par economic upswing. The proposed tax cuts totaling Danish krone (DKK) 23 billion \par (about $3 billion or 1% of GDP) are intended to be offset by expenditure \par reductions so as not to provide unnecessary fiscal stimulus to the economy. But \par the government might still face challenges in attaining the necessary \par parliamentary support. There is a possibility that the government won't be able \par to push through its expenditure containment plan, meaning further fiscal \par stimulus if the tax cuts are approved. On the other hand, the recent pension \par reform could extend the labor supply by adding flexibility to retirement \par savings, thereby increasing the incentive for the core labor force to remain \par active. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: A strong external position and low public \par debt provide ample flexibility \par      -- Very strong current account surpluses on the back of solid trade \par balances and robust investment revenues from overseas assets. \par      -- Low public debt provides strong fiscal flexibility, enabling public \par housing financing to move onto the central government balance sheet. \par      -- Predictable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  response patterns continue to stave off \par appreciation of the krone. \par  \par Denmark's external performance continues to enhance its net creditor position. \par For more than two decades, Denmark has posted current account surpluses, which \par were particularly strong in recent years, peaking at about 9% of GDP in 2015. \par We project that the current account surplus will average about 8% of GDP \par annually in 2017-2020, reflecting sound services, goods exports, and, in \par particular, robust net income from sizable external assets. We expect net \par exports will help the current account surplus surpass 8% of GDP in 2017, but \par that rising imports, as the economic cycle progresses, will gradually limit the \par trade balance's contribution to the current account. However, in 2018-2020, \par significant current income from Denmark's net wealth relative to other \par countries, as shown by a net external asset position of about 90% of current \par account receipts (CARs) at year-end 2016, will support the current account. \par  \par Using a narrower measure of Denmark's international investment position, we \par estimate that external debt, net of public- and financial-sector assets, will \par average 41% of CARs in 2017-2020, down from about 50% in 2016. We forecast \par current account payments plus short-term debt repayments will decline to about \par 180% of CARs plus official reserves in 2017, from 220% in 2010. Roughly \par three-fifths of Denmark's external debt is owed by its financial sector. \par  \par In this context, it is important to note that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  has decided to turn \par its Finnish subsidiary into a branch under the Danish headquarters. Moreover, \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nordea Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  has changed its legal structure by redefining its subsidiaries in \par Norway, Finland, and Denmark as branches and, more recently, has announced it \par will move its base to Finland in 2018. We note that Nordea Kredit Realkredit, \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nordea\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Danish covered bond entity, will remain in Denmark and hence its \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15 Sep 2017 16:11 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Denmark At -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par assets and liabilities will not be transferred to the head office. We do not \par anticipate any major impact from these changes on Denmark's external positions \par or gross external financing needs. \par  \par The krone is pegged to the euro, which, in our opinion, limits Denmark's \par monetary flexibility compared with that of sovereigns with floating and more \par actively traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and financial sectors that are better equipped to \par manage \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk. Following the pressure on Denmark's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg in the \par first half of 2015, monetary policy has gradually normalized, with the monetary \par policy rate spread to the eurozone currently negative 0.25 percentage points. \par Periods of international uncertainty typically increase investors' appetite for \par Danish krone, leading to appreciation pressures. We observe that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danmarks \par Nationalbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (DNB), \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Denmark\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market albeit to a modest extent. We expect the DNB will counteract \par any upward pressure on the krone in a similar fashion by increasing foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and moving its policy rate further into negative territory. \par  \par We consider Denmark's fiscal policy track record to be strong. For 2017 and \par 2018, we expect a headline fiscal deficit of 1.4% of GDP and 1.2% of GDP, \par respectively. We believe the current strong economic growth could allow for \par more rapid budgetary consolidation than envisaged in the proposed budget bill. \par  \par As a result of Denmark's budgetary performance, there is low government debt. \par In recent years, the government has reduced its treasury reserves, which we \par forecast will approach DKK100 billion by year-end 2017, equivalent to about 5% \par of GDP or just over one year of estimated bond redemptions. As such, we expect \par gross general government debt will remain below 40% of GDP through 2020, with \par net debt at about 24%, reflecting sizable liquid assets. In view of its solid \par financial position, the Danish government has recently proposed the possibility \par of financing public housing construction through onlending from the central \par treasury. This would change the current situation, in which public housing \par companies issue mortgage loans covered by public guarantees, predominantly from \par municipalities. We expect a decision in the second half of 2017, following \par which, yearly loan volumes of DKK20 billion could be added to the central \par government's funding requirement, with a corresponding reduction in mortgage \par bank lending. Although this change would translate into a direct liability of \par the central government, we consider that the government has ample room to \par manage a debt increase of this magnitude, given its currently low debt and \par consolidating fiscal trajectory. \par  \par We believe the situation in the Danish banking sector is stable and that it \par represents limited contingent liabilities to the sovereign. In addition, \par Denmark's implementation of the EU-wide Banking Recovery and Resolution \par Directive on June 1, 2015, has reduced the central government's contingent \par liabilities, in our view, since we expect the Danish authorities will use the \par bail-in tool to resolve deposit-taking banks, if necessary. The authorities \par also require mortgage lending institutions to hold a bail-in-able buffer of 2% \par of mortgage loans to avoid government bailouts of large systemic banking \par groups. The banking sector continues to record sound profitability on the back \par of lower loan losses and increased profitability of mortgage lending, while \par adhering to strict cost control. In particular, we note that impairment charges \par are currently at their lowest level since 2007, and evolving customer \par preferences toward digital solutions have allowed for reduced staff and \par branches. We expect some further expansion of the duration of wholesale funding \par as mortgage providers continue to transfer clients to longer-dated financing \par options to limit refinancing risk. But we note that the average duration of \par Danish bank's wholesale funding already exceeds that of Nordic peers by a wide \par margin. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Criteria - Governments - Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology - \par December 23,2014 \par      -- General Criteria: Guarantee Criteria - October 21,2016 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments - May 18,2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks - September 14,2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings - April 07,2017 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History - Sept. 8, 2017 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators - July 6, 2017. An interactive version is \par also available at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.spratings.com/sri}}}\uc2 \f2  \par      -- Credit Trends: 2016 Sovereign Ratings Update: Outlook And CreditWatch \par Resolutions - April 18, 2017 \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends: First-Quarter 2017 - April 10, 2017 \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2016 Annual Sovereign Default Study \par And Rating Transitions - April 3, 2017 \par      -- Sovereign Debt 2017: Global Borrowing To Drop By 4% To US$6.8 Trillion \par - Feb. 23, 2017 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate