\par\par \b \uc2 Euro Eases Franc's Strain -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Blackstone \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2017\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B8\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (July 8, 2017).\par \par \uc2 ZURICH -- The yearslong battle that smaller European central banks have waged against their own strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have turned a corner, thanks to the strengthening euro.\par \par \uc2 Increased confidence in the eurozone, both politically and economically, has raised the euro's value against a broad swath of big and small \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including some of its neighbors. Amid these improved prospects for Europe, central bankers in Switzerland, Denmark and the Czech Republic have dramatically scaled back their purchases of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves -- accumulated on a massive scale since 2012 -- dipped slightly last month to 693.5 billion Swiss francs ($721 billion), the SNB said Friday. The figures suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has pulled back on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets since March, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data. Data from the \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of just EUR653 million ($745 million) in April, down sharply from March.\par \par \uc2 This is in contrast to the first quarter, when these banks cumulatively added tens of billions of dollars' worth of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to their balance sheets. By creating new Swiss francs, Danish kroner and Czech koruna and using them to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets, these central banks hoped to weaken their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protect exporters while boosting inflation.\par \par \uc2 Their ability to step back is a byproduct of the broader normalization occurring among big central banks. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has raised rates twice this year and is expected to do so again before year-end. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reduced its quantitative easing program, and officials have sounded more positive about the bloc's economic outlook, even though its deposit rate is still negative.\par \par \uc2 This has the potential to reduce demand for assets in countries such as Switzerland, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tend to benefit amid uncertain times in the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "The Danes and Swiss are all at the mercy of large central banks. Sometimes they lose, like when the \uc2 ECB\uc2  cut rates into negative territory and sometimes it works in their favor," as is the case more recently, said Peter Rosenstreich, head of market strategy at \uc2 Swissquote Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Switzerland has battled the strong franc since the euro crisis intensified in 2010, prompting its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spend vast sums \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  and, eventually, setting a ceiling on the franc's strength in 2011. When it abandoned that cap in early 2015, it was forced to step up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile, has a target range for the euro-kroner exchange rate. The \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a euro target for a while, but dropped it in April.\par \par \uc2 The euro traded at just under 1.10 Swiss francs early Friday, near a one-year high. The euro fetched 7.44 kroner -- which is near the middle of the \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range -- and 26.1 Czech koruna, just slightly weaker than when the koruna target was in place.\par \par \uc2 A strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  brings many economic benefits. It reflects the confidence global investors attach to assets of countries such as Switzerland, which is known as a haven. It raises purchasing power and may make key imported commodities like oil less expensive.\par \par \uc2 But when it is too strong, the economy may slide into persistent consumer price declines known as deflation, which weakens consumption and investment. A strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also typically damps exports.\par \par \uc2 And the exchange rate effects extend beyond exports and consumer prices. The Czech Republic is a key manufacturing hub in sectors like automobiles and thus benefits from low costs. Switzerland is home to global corporate powerhouses like \uc2 Nestl\u233\'00\'E9 SA\uc2  and \uc2 Swatch Group AG\uc2  which generate a significant share of their revenue from overseas but report their earnings in francs.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is breathing a sigh of relief," said Mr. Rosenstreich.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brian Blackstone at brian.blackstone@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 08, 2017 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170708ed780004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
