\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Denmark \highlight\uc2  defends its  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  peg with biggest  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  since Brexit\b0\par\par\uc2 550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2017\par 19:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, comments, updates dateline)\par \par \uc2 By Teis Jensen and Jemima Kelly\par \par \uc2 COPENHAGEN/LONDON, March 2 (Reuters) - \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 4.7 billion Danish crowns ($665 million) on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in February to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg amid high demand for crowns from investors worried about the future of the euro.\par \par \uc2 It was the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June last year, when it stepped into the market as demand for the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose on fears that Britain's shock vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  could have ripple effects in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 Under the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM2), Denmark must keep the crown within 2.25 percent of 7.46038 crowns per euro, although in practice it has not moved more than 0.5 percent either way.\par \par \uc2 Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  faces political risks including a French presidential election which anti-euro, far-right leader Marine Le Pen is seen by bookmakers as having a 30 percent chance of winning.\par \par \uc2 This has prompted some investors to seek alternatives to the euro, and they see Denmark - among a handful of countries with a triple-A credit rating, and with a large surplus in its balance of payments - as a safe bet.\par \par \uc2 "If Le Pen wins, against expectations, she has announced that there will be a referendum on the euro - that would obviously shake the foundation of the euro cooperation, and that has led investors to seek shelter in Danish crowns," said Nykredit chief economist Tore Stramer, in Copenhagen.\par \par \uc2 "We will probably have to live with that traffic until (results day) in France on May 8."\par \par \uc2 With jitters over the French election weighing on the euro, the Danish crown in late February hit its highest levels since the results of June's Brexit vote, at 7.4325 crowns per euro .\par \par \uc2 "GREAT CONFIDENCE"\par \par \uc2 February's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was detailed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. It was dwarfed, however, by the hundreds of billions of crowns sold in early 2015 after the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  scrapped the ceiling on the Swiss franc's exchange rate against the euro, sparking a speculative run on the crown.\par \par \uc2 That \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bolstered investors' faith in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to defend the crown, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "There is a great confidence in the fixed exchange rate policy, which makes investors to some extent enforce (it) themselves because they don't want to buy crowns at too a high price, because they know (it) will fall back closer to the central rate again," said Sydbank economist Soren Kristensen.\par \par \uc2 He added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have fallen to levels last seen before the Swiss franc shock, would be willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  far more aggressively if strong upward pressure was put on the Danish crown. It would prefer to do that than further cut its key interest rate, currently at minus 0.65 percent, he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said its total \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to 466.6 billion Danish crowns in February, up from 457.8 billion crowns at the end of January.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 7.0687 Danish crowns) (Writing by Jemima Kelly; Editing by Catherine Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-3-2T19:27:55.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170302ed3200th9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
