\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Denmark \highlight\uc2  ready for lower rates,  \highlight22\uc2 interventions \highlight\uc2  to defend peg amid Brexit fears -c.bank\b0\par\par\uc2 370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2016\par 13:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds details, background)\par \par \uc2 By Teis Jensen\par \par \uc2 COPENHAGEN, June 15 (Reuters) - Denmark is ready to cut interest rates further into negative territory and make unlimited \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if needed to defend the crown's peg to the euro, the head of its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty over how Britain's referendum on June 23 on whether to stay in the \uc2 European Union\uc2  will play out has prompted investors to buy Danish crowns, seen as a safe haven.\par \par \uc2 That has helped strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the upper end of the tight band to the euro the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept it within.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in May for the first time in three months, buying foreign reserves and selling its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which helped ease pressure on the crown towards the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 The Nationalbank is ready for unlimited \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to let its reserve of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  "sky rocket", \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \uc2 Lars Rohde\uc2  told a press meeting on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 He said the bank could also cut interest rates further, a stronger tool used less often than \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the crown. In January the bank raised its certificate of deposit rate to minus 0.65 percent from minus 0.75 percent.\par \par \uc2 "We did not (reach) the lowest possible rate last year, so there is the possibility to go further into negative territory if needed," Rohde said.\par \par \uc2 Last year, speculators poured money into the AAA-rated country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , betting Denmark would drop the three-decade-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg and let the crown rise, after the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  scrapped its cap on the franc against the euro.\par \par \uc2 At 1108 GMT, the crown was trading at 7.4357 per euro .\par \par \uc2 Under the ERM2 Exchange Rate Mechanism, the crown must stay within 2.25 percent of a rate of 7.46038 per euro. In reality the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept to a much tighter range of plus/minus 0.50 percent. (Additional reporting by Erik Matzen and Jacob Gronholt-Pedersen,; editing by John Stonestreet) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-15T12:38:04.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160615ec6f00z9i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
