\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Danish  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  ready to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  if Brexit hits crown\b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2016\par 15:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 COPENHAGEN, June 3 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the crown if a British exit from the \uc2 European Union\uc2  later this month triggers inflows into the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Speculators poured money into AAA-rated country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January and February last year, betting Denmark would drop its three-decade-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg and let the crown rise, after the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  scrapped its cap on the franc against the euro.\par \par \uc2 Upward pressure on the Danish crown since early March has led the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy almost 24 billion crowns ($3.7 billion)worth of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May.\par \par \uc2 "We cannot rule out that an inflow of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can happen if the British vote no at the referendum," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Per Callesen said on the sidelines of a \uc2 Thomson Reuters\uc2  banking seminar in Copenhagen.\par \par \uc2 Callesen said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is standing firm on the current Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, which pegs the value of the crown to the euro.\par \par \uc2 "We don't see anyone in the political sphere or media pushing for a change in the regime. We believe we have the means available for keeping the exchange rate," Callesen said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is the tool the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses most to defend the peg. It has also raised the possibility of adjusting interest rates independently of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Callesen underscored that potential pressure on the crown would be different from what happened last year, when Switzerland changed its exchange rate regime.\par \par \uc2 "The referendum in Britain will not have any impact on the exchange regime," Callesen said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to 426.9 billion Danish crowns in May from 403.5 billion crowns a month earlier.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  marked by selling 23.6 billion crowns to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable against the euro. It was the first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since February.\par \par \uc2 Under the Exchange Rate Mechanism, \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2  must keep the crown within 2.25 percent of a rate of 7.46038 per euro. In practice, it has kept to a much tighter range of plus or minus 0.50 percent. ($1 = 6.5794 Danish crowns) (Reporting by \uc2 Ole Mikkelsen\uc2 ,; editing by Jacob Gronholt-Pedersen, Larry King)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-6T13:43:48.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160603ec63018zh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
