\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Denmark Intervened \highlight\uc2  As Brexit Fears Lifted DKK -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 159 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2016\par 17:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1508 GMT The \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it spent DKK23.6 billion in May to prevent the krone from appreciating, in accordance with its policy to keep EUR/DKK within a tight band. Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Danish assets have likely seen safe haven demand due to the risk of a U.K. vote to leave the EU, as well as Greece concerns. \uc2 Nordea\uc2  says much of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably occurred in the last two weeks of May, when EUR/DKK fell to around 7.436. It says a U.K. vote to leave the EU could "open the door to large capital inflows" into Denmark and trigger Danish rate cuts, "possibly even a Danish bond purchase programme." (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 02, 2016 11:08 ET (15:08 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160602ec62002g6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
