\par\par \b \uc2 NORDIC MORNING BRIEFING: Denmark Retail Sales Tops Thin Data Slate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dominic Chopping   \par \uc2 1,180 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2016\par 04:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR - All times GMT \par  \par Nordic Macro \par 0700 Apr Denmark retail sales \par  \par Other Macro \par N/A      G7 meeting of finance \par          ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central \par          bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors \par 1400 Apr US existing home sales \par  \par Other Equities \par Deere & Co 2Q \par Foot Locker 1Q \par  \par GLOBAL NEWS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Richmond Federal Reserve Bank President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jeffrey Lacker\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Thursday reiterated his support for an interest-rate increase at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy meeting in June, arguing that risks to the U.S. economy from outside have "entirely dissipated."\f28 \par \par \uc2 Mr. Lacker, in an interview with Bloomberg Radio, said he sees "risks from global and financial developments having virtually entirely dissipated," allowing for a "very strong" case for raising rates at the June 14-15 meeting of the interest-rate-setting Federal Open Market Committee. Mr. Lacker isn't a voting member of the FOMC this year. Mr. Lacker added that he supported a rate increase in April, when the Fed held rates steady.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lacker told Bloomberg Radio that financial markets had taken "the wrong signal from us" after the Fed paused from raising rates in March and April.\par \par \uc2 "They overestimated how likely we were to pause for the rest of the year, " Mr. Lacker said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Union\uc2  's economic sanctions against Russia look set to be extended beyond July because of the continuing conflict in Ukraine, the bloc's foreign policy chief said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Federica Mogherini's comments, in an interview with German newspaper Die Welt, come after European and U.S. diplomats said they were increasingly confident Western economic pressure on Russia over its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine would continue.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 EU\uc2  imposed economic sanctions on Russia in two tranches in the summer of 2014 in response to Moscow's annexation of Ukraine's Crimea region and its support for separatists in eastern Ukraine. Russia has denied Western charges that it supplied and directed the rebels.\par \par \uc2 The sanctions, which expire in late July, include restrictions on defense and energy business ties. They also prevent state-owned banks, energy and defense companies from raising money in European financial markets. Washington has adopted similar measures.\par \par \uc2 A top \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  official was due to say Thursday that he will consider the case for more stimulus if the U.K. economy doesn't improve later this year, even if voters choose to stay in the \uc2 European Union\uc2  in a referendum on membership in June.\par \par \uc2 Gertjan Vlieghe, one of the nine members of the BOE's Monetary Policy Committee, planned to say in a speech in London that the U.K. economy has lost momentum over the past three years, amid disappointing global growth, a squeeze on British public spending and poor productivity growth. Annual inflation, measured in April at 0.3%, remains well below the BOE's 2% goal.\par \par \uc2 He planned to say that uncertainty over the outcome of a referendum on the U.K.'s \uc2 EU\uc2  membership, scheduled for June 23, is also contributing to the slowdown, primarily by delaying spending and investment. But he said officials are struggling to untangle how much of the cooling-off reflects these short-term effects and how much is due to more persistent problems.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vlieghe also planned to say that he would like to see "convincing evidence of an improvement in the economic outlook" after June's vote, assuming Britons side with Prime Minister \uc2 David Cameron\uc2  and choose to stay in, according to a text of his prepared remarks.\par \par \uc2 "If such an improvement is not apparent soon it will reduce my confidence that inflation is likely to return to the target within an acceptable time horizon without additional monetary stimulus," he planned to say in an address at \uc2 London Business School\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The U.S. and its European partners made a fresh appeal Thursday for European banks and businesses to invest in Iran, saying they would do more to encourage legitimate business in the country now that most sanctions are gone.\par \par \uc2 Following a meeting in Brussels between U.S. Secretary of State \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  , European Union foreign policy chief Federica Mogherini and their counterparts from France, Germany and the U.K., the governments pledged to provide more information and assistance to encourage companies to resume business ties with Tehran. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A thin calendar Friday sees Danish retail sales before attention turns to U.S. existing home sales later.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "While existing home sales have trended sideways over the past year or so, sales rebounded strongly in March and are expected to continue to rise in April," says SEB.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEB noteD that fundamentals for housing in the U.S. continue to look good, but sales are constrained by the limited inventory of homes for sale. The low supply is pushing prices higher, in turn depressing sales activity, but on the other hand, the labor market is getting ever stronger which means that incomes are improving, SEB said. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par 0250 GMT 9.3488-538 \par Previous 9.3617-67 \par %Chg -0.14 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par 0250 GMT 9.3487-587 \par Previous 9.3581-681 \par %Chg -0.10 \par  \par USD/NOK \par 0250 GMT 8.3423-83 \par Previous 8.3558-618 \par %Chg -0.16 \par  \par USD/SEK \par 0250 GMT 8.3437-527 \par Previous 8.3534-624 \par %Chg -0.12 \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EUR/DKK fell below 7.4370 Wednesday, noted \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , and it expects the cross to trade at 7.4375 on a 12 month horizon, down from a previous estimate of 7.4425.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Danske\f28 \uc2 \f2  thinks downside risk beyond 7.4350 would be capped by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Denmark\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  . "We look for DKK to stay supported by a tighter policy rate spread to \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  , a low net position, hedging of potential EUR risk due to the U.K. EU referendum in June and in the near term, foreigners buying DKK at the Dong IPO." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40  1411.6 -0.98% \par OMXS30  1321.8 -1.43% \par OBX     533.28 -1.92% \par OMXC20 1010.73 -0.17% \par OMXH25    3074 -0.92% \par  \par Brent crude: $49.08 \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordic markets closed mostly lower Thursday as investors digested the latest \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  minutes that suggested interest rates could rise as early as next month if economic data is supportive. The comments fuelled a further rally in the U.S. dollar, weighing on commodity-based companies in the region which were among the worst-performing stocks on the day. The price of crude pulled back sharply, dragging on Oslo's oil-heavy OBX index. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil prices edged higher in early Asia trade, shrugging off the modest firming in the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices settled with little change in New York trading hours on Thursday. Around 0159 GMT, front-month Brent crude remained within striking distance of $50, notching a gain of 27 cents to $49.08 per barrel. Light, sweet crude in New York was trading up 39 cents at $48.55/barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Dominic Chopping at dominic.chopping@wsj.com; Twitter: @domchopping @WSJNordics\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 19, 2016 22:58 ET (02:58 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160520ec5k0007s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
