\par\par \b \uc2 Danish rate hike may be coming after  \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ole Mikkelsen   \par \uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2015\par 14:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 COPENHAGEN, Dec 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent 25 billion to 30 billion crowns ($4.4 billion) this month to maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the euro, an effort that may trigger a rise in interest rates, perhaps as early as Thursday, some analysts said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  linked to the euro led it to slash rates and sell crowns earlier this year to weaken the crown. Lately, though, it has had to buy crowns to keep its value closer to the euro's. A rate increase, which would also strengthen the crown, would be the logical next move.\par \par \uc2 Even the modest increase expected by most analysts would move Danish monetary policy in the opposite direction to the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's. \uc2 The ECB\uc2  cut its deposit rate by 0.10 percentage point earlier in December.\par \par \uc2 Because the Danish crown is linked to the euro in a narrow range, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually conducts the same monetary policy as \uc2 the ECB\uc2  and normally changes rates on the same day as \uc2 the ECB\uc2 . But it sat out \uc2 the ECB\uc2 's Dec. 3 rate cut.\par \par \uc2 Since \uc2 the ECB\uc2  rate decision, analysts estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent more of its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, meaning it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to strengthen the crown.\par \par \uc2 "We now forecast that over the next three months the \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hike the certificates of deposit rate by a modest 10 basis points to -0.65 percent," \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  wrote.\par \par \uc2 "An independent rate hike could potentially come as early as today," it wrote in a note to clients on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no scheduled rate-setting meetings. But rate changes tend to be announced on Thursdays at 1500 GMT.\par \par \uc2 Chief economist Jes Asmussen at Handelsbanken Markets estimates the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 30 billion crowns. Nordea Markets puts it at 25 billion.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves might be back on pre-crisis levels within the next couple of months, which may open up for an independent Danish rate hike," \uc2 Nordea\uc2  wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 Denmark's certificate of deposit rate has been deep in negative territory since early February, at -0.75 percent. \uc2 The ECB\uc2  lowered its deposit rate by 10 basis points to -0.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Four interest rate cuts in January and February to keep the crown within its euro range after strong pressure on the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has resulted in record low rates.\par \par \uc2 To keep at bay speculators betting Denmark will scrap the euro peg, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  flooded the market with crowns, building up foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. It also called off government bond issues to ease demand for Danish assets.\par \par \uc2 Its reserves peaked at a record of 737 billion Danish crowns ($107 billion) in March. By the end of November, reserves amounted to 484 billion crowns, not far off the at 447 billion seen at the end of last year, before the pressure on the crown.\par \par \uc2 Under the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's Exchange Rate Mechanism, Denmark, an \uc2 EU\uc2  member but euro zone outsider, has agreed to keep the crown at 2.25 percent either side of the parity rate of 7.46038 crowns to the euro. In practice, the crown has not moved more than 0.5 percent on either side. ($1 = 6.8779 Danish crowns) (Editing by Sabina Zawadzki, Larry King) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-12-17T14:46:51.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151217ebch00h3y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
