\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Denmark \highlight\uc2  Did Not  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange \highlight\uc2  Market in March--2nd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2015\par 19:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Revises first two paragraphs.)\par \par \uc2 By Charles Duxbury\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday that it refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in March, in a sign that the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stabilized against the euro following weeks of volatility brought on by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stimulus efforts.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been battling to stabilize the exchange rate, its core task, since the ECB announced a massive bond-buying stimulus program in January and sent the euro plummeting against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the krone.\par \par \uc2 Nationalbanken said its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves remained at an elevated 737.1 billion kroner ($107.2 billion) after it sold a record amount of its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January and February to weaken it but that it had stayed out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last month.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been battling to stabilize the exchange rate, its core task, since the ECB kicked off a massive bond-buying stimulus program earlier this year and sent the euro plummeting against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the krone.\par \par \uc2 As well as selling kroner, Denmark cut interest rates four times earlier this year and suspended bond issuance to sap interest in the krone among investors seeking better returns than they could get in the eurozone.\par \par \uc2 That interest rose sharply after Switzerland abandoned its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ceiling to the euro as the ECB bond program loomed and traders began wondering if Denmark could be next.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's only policy aim is to keep the euro's exchange rate within 2.25% above or below 7.46038 kroner to keep a lid on inflation and provide stability for domestic businesses that look to the eurozone as an important export market. It has no inflation target.\par \par \uc2 The peg has existed in close to its current form for more than three decades, first relative to the German \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and later against the euro.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Lars Rohde\uc2  has described the peg as the cornerstone of Danish economic policy and said the bank will do whatever it takes to defend it.\par \par \uc2 The ceasing of kroner sales in March is the latest sign that Nationalbanken's efforts have worked. Last Monday it issued short-term debt for the first time in two months.\par \par \uc2 Write to Charles Duxbury at charles.duxbury@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 07, 2015 13:11 ET (17:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150407eb47002c4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
