\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL CENTRAL BANKS: Denmark May See Krone's Tumult Fade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Charles Duxbury   \par \uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2015\par 11:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been under huge pressure to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the euro, its main policy aim, but recent signs are that the storm could be passing.\par \par \uc2 In its most recent statement on its market activities, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the latter part of February after buying record amounts of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January and early February to sap demand for the Danish krone.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main policy goal is to keep the euro's exchange rate within 2.25% above or below 7.46038 kroner to keep a lid on inflation and provide stability for domestic businesses that rely on the eurozone as a market.\par \par \uc2 That task was made harder earlier this year by Switzerland's move to abandon its exchange-rate cap which got investors wondering whether Denmark could be next. Investors began buying up krone assets, pushing up its value, knowing that if the peg broke those assets would be worth more in euro terms.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bond-buying plan has also pushed down the value of the euro against the krone, forcing \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates four times and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  extensively in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With ECB asset-buying set to run for a protracted period, the \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be on alert for moves higher in the krone's value compared with the euro.\par \par \uc2 If the current period of calmer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets continues, analysts expect Nationalbanken to keep rates unchanged for a period before gradually raising the deposit rate back toward zero.\par \par \uc2 However, if the krone starts strengthening again, Nationalbanken has made clear it is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and cut interest rates as necessary to maintain the peg.\par \par \uc2 "We are able and we are willing to do whatever it takes to defend the peg," Gov. \uc2 Lars Rohde\uc2  said recently. "The peg is the cornerstone of economic policy in Denmark and has very broad based support."\par \par \uc2 --Write to Charles Duxbury at charles.duxbury@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This article was corrected at 14:33 GMT to fix the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the healine. It's krone, not krune.\par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2015 09:31 ET  Correction to Outlook for \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Charles Duxbury \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (The article "GLOBAL CENTRAL BANKS: Denmark May See Krune's Tumult Fades, published at 5:59 a.m. EDT and 6:04 EDT, erroneously referred to Denmark's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a the krune in the headline. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is named the krone.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 02, 2015 09:31 ET (13:31 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150402eb42001w4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
