\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Central bank \highlight\uc2  fightback helps Danish crown to 14-year low\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag   \par \uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2015\par 13:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 1 (Reuters) - The Danish crown fell to its weakest against the euro in 14 years on Wednesday, marking a victory for \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its battle to defend the three-decade-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.\par \par \uc2 Speculators have been betting Denmark will abandon the peg and let the crown rise since the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  dropped the franc's cap against the euro on Jan. 15 and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced its 1 trillion euro stimulus programme.\par \par \uc2 That compelled the \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slash interest rates four times in January and February, and it has spent an estimated $40 billion since then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets.\par \par \uc2 "Those speculative flows are being unwound," said Richard Falkenhall, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at SEB. "It looks like the \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been successful in defending the peg and the upward pressure on the crown is easing."\par \par \uc2 In robust volumes on the Thomson Reuters trading platform, the Danish crown fell to 7.4720 per euro early on Wednesday, its lowest against the euro since late 2000.\par \par \uc2 It was trading at 7.4704 per euro at 1100 GMT, with some traders saying Danish company dividend payments to foreign investors were causing the crown to weaken.\par \par \uc2 The crown had struck a 10-year high of 7.43 crowns per euro on Jan. 16, the day after the SNB removed its 1.20 per euro cap on the franc, triggering a 40 percent surge in the Swiss unit.\par \par \uc2 The steady drop seen over the last few days also meant the crown's first quarter losses were the worst in any quarter since the euro was introduced in 1999. The peg usually limits movements in the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a tiny 0.01 percent per day.\par \par \uc2 Since January, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has several times restated its commitment to arrangements under the Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM II), which requires the crown to be kept within a range of 2.25 percent either side of 7.46038 crowns per euro. In practice, it has been kept within 0.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 In the pre-euro era, the crown was pegged to the German mark, starting in 1982.\par \par \uc2 With pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ebbing, analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to loosen monetary policy or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further. \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2  will publish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve data on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "There won't be any rate cuts coming in the next few months," said Steen Bocian, chief economist at \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-4-1T12:26:28.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150401eb4100d9q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
