\par\par \uc2 Top Stories\par \b \uc2 Denmark  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Raises Current-Account Ceiling\b0\par\par\uc2 By CHARLES DUXBURY   \par \uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 13:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Global Market Stories\par \uc2 DJTGMS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 AARHUS--\uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved to ease the financial burden its negative deposit rate has placed on commercial lenders as it increased the amount of money banks could hold in its overnight current account.\par \par \uc2 Nationalbanken said on Wednesday that banks could now hold 145 billion Danish kroner ($20.65 billion) in excess liquidity in its current account compared with 37 billion kroner earlier.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account has an interest rate of zero while money deposited at the seven-day certificates-of-deposit rate incurs a charge of 0.75%.\par \par \uc2 As of Tuesday, Danish banks had 29 billion kroner in the current account and 347.3 billion kroner in certificates of deposit. Wednesday's changes allow banks to move a significant amount of their excess liquidity to the current account and this will ease some of the financial pressure they have been under since the introduction of the negative-deposit rate, analysts at Danish lender \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 This negative deposit rate is one of the weapons deployed this year by \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its battle to protect its long-held \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg with the euro. Cutting rates so low makes krone-denominated assets less attractive to investors and weakens the value of the krone.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main policy goal is to keep the euro's exchange rate within 2.25% above or below 7.46038 kroner to keep a lid on inflation and provide stability for domestic businesses that look to the eurozone as an important export market.\par \par \uc2 That task was made harder earlier this year by the Swiss move to abandon its exchange-rate cap which got investors wondering whether Denmark could be next. Investors began buying up krone assets knowing that if the peg broke those assets would be worth more in euro terms.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive bond-buying plan has also pushed down the value of the euro against the krone, forcing \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates four times and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  extensively in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was crucial that any changes it makes to its policies didn't affect the transmission of its negative interest rate on deposits to money market rates. It said it had judged that the changes to the current account terms wouldn't do that.\par \par \uc2 Write to Charles Duxbury at charles.duxbury@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTGMS1120150318eb3i0006x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
