\par\par \b \uc2 NORDIC MORNING BRIEFING: Focus on Eurozone PMI, U.S. \uc2 ADP\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jens Hansegard   \par \uc2 834 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 06:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR - All times GMT \par  \par Other Macro \par 0900 Eurozone Services PMI Feb \par 1000 Eurozone Retail Sales Jan \par 1315 U.S. ADP Jobs Data Feb \par  \par Nordic Equities \par 0630 Elekta Earnings \par 0700 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Subsea 7\f28 \uc2 \f2  Earnings \par N/A \f28 \uc2 \f2 DS Norden\f28 \uc2 \f2  Earnings \par N/A Torm Earnings \par  \par  \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earnings season is drawing to a close in the Nordics and on Wednesday investors will focus on Eurozone services PMIs, expected to confirm a welcome return to European expansion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 European retail sales in January are seen somewhat higher on the month. Still, European consumers remain slightly hampered by a lack of credit, which is one of the reasons for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive new monetary stimulus program.\par \par \uc2 In the afternoon, focus turns to U.S. jobs data from \uc2 ADP\uc2 , as investors look for clues ahead of Friday's U.S. jobs report, to see if January's strong numbers will be repeated. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par 0450 GMT  8.6135-85 \par Previous  8.6166-216 \par %Chg -0.04 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par 0450 GMT 9.2430-530 \par Previous 9.2429-529 \par %Chg  +0.00 \par  \par USD/NOK \par 0450 GMT 7.7121-81 \par Previous 7.7096-156 \par %Chg +0.03 \par  \par USD/SEK \par 0450 GMT 8.2754-844 \par Previous 8.2707-97 \par %Chg  +0.06 \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swedish krona traded up Tuesday, reaching one-month highs against the euro and the Norwegian krone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the moves are on a combination of hawkish comments from Riksbank Governor Ingves that the Swedish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is rather weak and that inflation will rise soon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strong German January retail sales, up 2.9% on the month versus expectations of a 0.3% decline are good news for countries, like Sweden, that export to Germany, BNP said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEB said that data so far this year has not portended any sharply weaker economic momentum for Norway and has rather been positive for the Norwegian krone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "However, we think data is about to turn weaker, which should put upside pressure on EUR/NOK in the short-term," SEB said, adding that the extent of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's dovishness will depend on the oil investment survey and the regional network report due later this week. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40 1,599.65 -0.8% \par OMXS30 1,661.87 -1.5% \par OBX      560.11 +0.7% \par OMXC20   873.13 -0.7% \par OMXH25 3,427.39 -0.7% \par  \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordic markets closed mixed Tuesday, amid caution ahead of a \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  monetary policy meeting later this week from which investors will look for answers about the scope of the ECB's bond-buying program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oslo's energy-heavy OBX bucked the wider European cautious trend by gaining 0.7% as oil prices gained on concerns about violence in Libya and expectations that crude-oil stockpile builds could be slowing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With little corporate news on Wednesday's agenda, investors will look at the U.S. \f28 \uc2 \f2 ADP\f28 \uc2 \f2  jobs data for clues on the U.S. labor market ahead of Friday's non-farm payrolls release. Friday's February jobs report is expected to show the economy added 240,000 new jobs, according to economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude-oil futures were mixed in early Asian trade after gains ahead of Wednesday's U.S. oil inventory data from the Department of Energy. According to a survey of analysts by The Wall Street Journal, U.S. crude-oil stocks are expected to have risen by 4.6 million barrels in the week ended Feb. 27.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Tuesday, API data showed U.S. crude supplies rose by 2.9 million barrels. Oil prices have moved within the $58-$60 a barrel range in the last few weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Brent prices were supported by ongoing unrest in Libya, reviving supply fears from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  member," ANZ said. Libyan oil production has jumped in recent days despite fresh attacks on fields and an air raid near a terminal, a top official said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Saudi Arabia also increased its official selling price for buyers in Asia, the U.S. and northwest Europe," ANZ said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0210 GMT, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  WTI crude was up 10 cents at $50.62 and Brent crude was down 24 cents at $60.78. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEWS ROUNDUP: \par  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 DENMARK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Denmark\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in February, in line with its long-running efforts to prevent the krone from climbing too high and to maintain its fixed exchange rate with the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Network operator \f28 \uc2 \f2 Millicom International Cellular SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MIC-SDB.SK) said Tuesday it has appointed Mauricio Ramos as its new chief executive effective from April 1.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: The Norwegian Nobel Committee, which decides who is awarded the prestigious Nobel Peace Prize, on Tuesday replaced its chairman, Thorbj\u248\'00\'F8rn Jagland, whose tenure has been marked by controversy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Jens Hansegard at jens.hansegard@wsj.com; Twitter: @WSJNordics\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 04, 2015 00:43 ET (05:43 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150304eb34000kv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
