\par\par \uc2 Top Stories\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Denmark Intervenes \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Market in a Big Way\b0\par\par\uc2 By BRIAN BLACKSTONE And EMESE BARTHA   \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 18:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top North American Financial Services Stories\par \uc2 DJTNAF\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a record amount of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month in a bid to protect the krone's peg to the euro, underscoring the fallout from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to boost economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The Nationalbanken's net purchases of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounted to 168.7 billion Danish kronor ($25.32 billion) in February, equal to about 9% of the Nordic country's gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 The scale of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a record for \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which also made significant net purchases of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the previous month. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't specify the types of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  it buys.\par \par \uc2 "The [\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] has never bought or sold \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  to this extent before, which shows that the krone has been under very significant appreciation pressure in recent months," said \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economist Tina Mortensen in a note.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the bulk of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place during days around its interest-rate reduction on Feb. 5. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves increased by 172.9 billion kronor, to 737.1 billion kronor in February.\par \par \uc2 Even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tapered off in February, "it is too early to say that the battle is over," said Nomura analyst Jens Nordvig, in a research note. "The \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may need to consider extreme measures to reinforce the peg further" such as temporary capital controls or transaction taxes, he wrote.\par \par \uc2 A representative for \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  told The Wall Street Journal last month that the bank is standing firm on the peg. "The peg is a cornerstone of Danish economic policy and has been so since 1982, and there is a broad commitment throughout Denmark for the fixed exchange rate," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to keep the euro's exchange rate within 2.25% above or below 7.46038 kroner to keep a lid on inflation and provide stability for domestic businesses that look to the eurozone as an important export market. The peg has existed in close to its current form for more than three decades, first relative to the German \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and later against the euro.\par \par \uc2 But the peg has come under pressure as investors poured funds into kroner recently in search of better returns than they can get in the eurozone. \uc2 The ECB\uc2 's planned bond-buying program has pushed eurozone bond yields down and led to a decline in the value of the euro. Bond yields move inversely to prices. Denmark has cut its key policy rates four times this year, with the certificate of deposit rate most recently reduced by 0.25 percentage point to minus 0.75%. When monetary authorities set a negative interest rate on bank deposits, financial institutions, in effect, pay a fee to park their excess funds with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In other measures aimed at deflecting foreign inflows, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has suspended government bond issuance and rejected bids at some recent treasury auctions.\par \par \uc2 The ECB\uc2 's decision in January to purchase more than EUR1 trillion ($1.12 trillion) in public and private bonds has put significant upward pressure on haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside the eurozone, including the krone and the Swiss franc.\par \par \uc2 In January, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  abandoned the ceiling it had set on the franc's value against the euro, as defending that cap had become too costly. Like the \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has been charging banks to hold their deposits to limit foreign capital flows into its economy.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which also isn't part of the eurozone, cut its main interest rate into negative territory and launched a bond-purchase program.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brian Blackstone at brian.blackstone@wsj.com and Emese Bartha at emese.bartha@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTNAF1120150303eb330000y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
