\par\par \b \uc2 A 'Real Food Fight' for New \uc2 Actavis\uc2  Bonds -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 21:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 15:05 EST - Prices on \uc2 Actavis\uc2 ' (ACT) new bonds are higher as investors seek to get a piece of the deal. At $21B, the sale was the 2nd-larget corporate-debt offering, but there were about $90B in orders. It's a "real food fight," one investor says. Yields on the bonds are some 0.15 percentage point or more lower than where the bonds priced earlier today, according to investors. (mike.cherney@wsj.com; @Mike_Cherney)\par \par \uc2 14:52 EST - BlackRock's (BLK) iShares Core US Aggregate Bond ETF (AGG) logged a record daily net outflow yesterday at $525.8M, the giant money manager notes, but it doesn't call the move surprising. It says large inflows and outflows can be seen in bond ETFs at a time of changing interest-rate expectations. The world's 2nd-largest bond ETF saw its net asset value fall 0.49% today after news of the outflow was reported by Bloomberg. Fund tracker Lipper also said the outflow wasn't surprising, noting the $24B ETF had $101M of net outflows on Feb. 13. AGG is down 4c at $110.41.(katy.burne@wsj.com; @katyburne)\par \par \uc2 14:47 EST - Eurasia Group returned from Caracas convinced that government was committed to paying bondholders. Eurasia says cash-strapped government is looking to repeat debt-exchange deal it struck with Dominican Republic with other Petrocaribe members. The most natural candidate would be Jamaica, which owes $2.9B. Eurasia says Venezuela is also seeking loans from Qatar, Russia, Saudi Arabia and China. The government will have to continue cutting back imports, however, after a 22% contraction last year, Eurasia says. If it does, it will risk unrest among a population already enduring shortages. (ezequiel.minaya@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:59 EST - The New Zealand dollar has gotten a lift against the greenback from the steady GlobalDairyTrade auction, where average prices rose 1.1% from the prior event. The kiwi is at US$0.7565 early in New Zealand trading, and BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Kymberly Martin notes resistance as the pair nears US$0.7610. Support is seen around US$0.7500. She adds a lack of domestic data mean attention will likely focus on Australia's 4Q GDP, due Wednesday afternoon. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 13:22 EST - Fitch says struggling US coal miners are unlikely to swoop in and repurchase some of their crushing debt despite high interest payments and bonds trading at cut-rate prices. The likes of Arch (ACI) and Alpha Natural (ANR) have debt trading at distressed levels with steep discounts. Some competitors have offered stock, cash and secured notes to buy back that kind of debt on the cheap, but don't expect that to become a trend, the ratings firm predicts. Some miners have built up cash piles to help survive low coal prices and they are lowering their costs to keep running and supplying their customers. Those assets are worth defending and these companies won't "easily sacrifice liquidity" to buy back their notes, Fitch says. (tim.puko@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1822 GMT Austria's credit rating won't be immediately affected by a moratorium on payments for Heta Asset Resolution AG's financial debt, the rating agency \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  says. The moratorium was announced on Sunday and affects almost all of Heta's financial debt until May 2016. Heta is the wind-down entity set up for the former lender \uc2 Hypo Alpe-Adria-Bank International AG\uc2 . \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  says the Austrian government has made comments that it will continue to service the EUR1 billion in debt covered by Austrian federal government guarantees. \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  rates Austria's long-term debt at AA+.(nicole.lundeen@wsj.com, @nicole_lundeen) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:59 EST - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Actavis\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' bond sale shows the hunger for yield is still strong. But investors ought to look to both sides of the corporate capital structure. The generic pharmaceutical maker on Tuesday closed a $21B debt deal. Proceeds will be used to finance the cash portion of its acquisition of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Allergan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , announced in November. Investors starved for fresh investment-grade debt were eager for a piece: orders reportedly exceeded $90B. But in this interest-rate environment, income doesn't just have to come from bonds. While \f28 \uc2 \f2 Actavis\f28 \uc2 \f2  doesn't pay a dividend and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Allergan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's payout is miniscule, there are five U.S. pharma majors--Abbvie, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bristol-Myers Squibb\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eli Lilly\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Merck and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pfizer\f28 \uc2 \f2 --that offer an average dividend yield of just under 3%. That is roughly 0.9 percentage points higher than what the 10-year Treasury offers, and about matches the yield of the five-year, fixed-rate tranche offered in Tuesday's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Actavis\f28 \uc2 \f2  deal. Stocks and bonds alike can experience capital losses, of course. But nowadays, investors searching for income have to consider all their options. (charles.grant@dowjones.com; @CGrantWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:37 EST - While proposing higher personal-income and sales taxes and lower corporate-income taxes as part of his first budget proposal, Pennsylvania Gov. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tom Wolf\f28 \uc2 \f2  tells state legislators he wants to raise the state's minimum wage to $10.10/hour from the current $7.25. He also wants to increase training and apprenticeship programs for workers so they can get higher-paying jobs in the state's shale-gas industry and in manufacturing. Additionally, Wolf proposed a tax break for manufacturing companies which increase annual taxable payroll by at least $1M; he envisions such firms receiving cash payments of up to 5% of new taxable income the following year. (kris.maher@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:05 EST - Brazilian consumers lead the world in adoption of online shopping, according to a new survey conducted by comScore on behalf of \f28 \uc2 \f2 UPS\f28 \uc2 \f2 . About 56% of their purchases are made online, the highest of any market in the survey. They're also patient: 38% are willing to wait more than 10 days to qualify for free shipping for international orders. Asian consumers were the least willing to wait for an order, with 27% saying they expected same-day delivery and nearly half expecting a retailer to offer next-day shipping. (laura.stevens@WSJ.com; @LauraStevensWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:36 EST - So-called middle-market companies (those with $50-500M of annual sales) contribute $1.7T to the US economy, or 13% of output, and support 16.5M American jobs, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Among 15 major global markets examined, the US was found to have the largest concentration of middle-market companies at 55,700, followed by China at 40,200. They generate 74M jobs. In the US, more than 1/3 of middle-market businesses are in the wholesale and retail sectors, followed by business services such as legal, accounting and design. (erica.phillips@wsj.com; @EEPhillips_WSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1636 GMT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Denmark\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hard to defend its EUR/DKK peg, making \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases of DKK168.7 billion in February. This pushes its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve to an all-time high of DKK737 billion. Flows may have moderated in the last few weeks, but it is too early to say the battle is over, says Nomura in a note. Indeed, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may need to consider extreme measures to reinforce the peg further, with temporary capital controls and transaction taxes two potential options left. Capital controls are controversial in the context of the EU's rules on free capital mobility, says Nomura, but transaction taxes would be consistent with what major EU countries are already planning for implementation in 2016. (peter.nurse@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1635 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The scale of Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in February is "the highest amount since the introduction of the euro," notes analyst Jan Storup Nielsen at Nordea. "Even though the pressure for a stronger DKK seems to have diminished over the last week it is still way too early for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to consider a rate hike. This will probably only been done once the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gets more information on how the upcoming QE-program will affect the Danish market. Towards year-end we expect the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to do the first rate hike." (Katie.martin@wsj.com, @katie_martin_\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 fx\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 03, 2015 15:05 ET (20:05 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150303eb33003l1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\