\par\par \b \uc2 Actavis\uc2  Bond Buyers Should Look to Stocks As Well -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 18:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:59 EST - \uc2 Actavis\uc2 ' bond sale shows the hunger for yield is still strong. But investors ought to look to both sides of the corporate capital structure. The generic pharmaceutical maker on Tuesday closed a $21B debt deal. Proceeds will be used to finance the cash portion of its acquisition of \uc2 Allergan\uc2 , announced in November. Investors starved for fresh investment-grade debt were eager for a piece: orders reportedly exceeded $90B. But in this interest-rate environment, income doesn't just have to come from bonds. While \uc2 Actavis\uc2  doesn't pay a dividend and \uc2 Allergan\uc2 's payout is miniscule, there are five U.S. pharma majors--Abbvie, \uc2 Bristol-Myers Squibb\uc2 , \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2 , Merck and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 --that offer an average dividend yield of just under 3%. That is roughly 0.9 percentage points higher than what the 10-year Treasury offers, and about matches the yield of the five-year, fixed-rate tranche offered in Tuesday's \uc2 Actavis\uc2  deal. Stocks and bonds alike can experience capital losses, of course.\par \par \uc2  But nowadays, investors searching for income have to consider all their options. (charles.grant@dowjones.com; @CGrantWSJ)\par \par \uc2 12:37 EST - While proposing higher personal-income and sales taxes and lower corporate-income taxes as part of his first budget proposal, Pennsylvania Gov. \uc2 Tom Wolf\uc2  tells state legislators he wants to raise the state's minimum wage to $10.10/hour from the current $7.25. He also wants to increase training and apprenticeship programs for workers so they can get higher-paying jobs in the state's shale-gas industry and in manufacturing. Additionally, Wolf proposed a tax break for manufacturing companies which increase annual taxable payroll by at least $1M; he envisions such firms receiving cash payments of up to 5% of new taxable income the following year. (kris.maher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:05 EST - Brazilian consumers lead the world in adoption of online shopping, according to a new survey conducted by comScore on behalf of \uc2 UPS\uc2 . About 56% of their purchases are made online, the highest of any market in the survey. They're also patient: 38% are willing to wait more than 10 days to qualify for free shipping for international orders. Asian consumers were the least willing to wait for an order, with 27% saying they expected same-day delivery and nearly half expecting a retailer to offer next-day shipping. (laura.stevens@WSJ.com; @LauraStevensWSJ)\par \par \uc2 11:36 EST - So-called middle-market companies (those with $50-500M of annual sales) contribute $1.7T to the US economy, or 13% of output, and support 16.5M American jobs, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . Among 15 major global markets examined, the US was found to have the largest concentration of middle-market companies at 55,700, followed by China at 40,200. They generate 74M jobs. In the US, more than 1/3 of middle-market businesses are in the wholesale and retail sectors, followed by business services such as legal, accounting and design. (erica.phillips@wsj.com; @EEPhillips_WSJ)\par \par \uc2 1636 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  hard to defend its EUR/DKK peg, making \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of DKK168.7 billion in February. This pushes its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve to an all-time high of DKK737 billion. Flows may have moderated in the last few weeks, but it is too early to say the battle is over, says Nomura in a note. Indeed, the \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may need to consider extreme measures to reinforce the peg further, with temporary capital controls and transaction taxes two potential options left. Capital controls are controversial in the context of the EU's rules on free capital mobility, says Nomura, but transaction taxes would be consistent with what major EU countries are already planning for implementation in 2016. (peter.nurse@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1635 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The scale of Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in February is "the highest amount since the introduction of the euro," notes analyst Jan Storup Nielsen at Nordea. "Even though the pressure for a stronger DKK seems to have diminished over the last week it is still way too early for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to consider a rate hike. This will probably only been done once the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gets more information on how the upcoming QE-program will affect the Danish market. Towards year-end we expect the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to do the first rate hike." (Katie.martin@wsj.com, @katie_martin_\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 fx\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1633 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strongly in February to defend its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg, with purchases for DKK168.7 billion in February, according to data released today. "Despite the surge in the Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve, it is a fair distance from the size of reserves in Hong Kong, Singapore and Switzerland," strategists at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  say. The bank expects the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep EUR/DKK in the range of 7.4440-7.4700 in the next months and not to make changes to the key policy rate within the coming 12 months. EUR/DKK is now at 7.452. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1629 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--"With low inflation, you can buy more stuff," European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  famously said in June 2013. Germany appears to have taken him at his word. German retail sales jumped 2.9% on the month in January and 5.3% on the year, far in excess of expectations. This certainly goes against the stereotype of Germany's ultra-cautious consumers, unlike the avid shoppers of the U.S. and U.K, and may not be a flash in the pan. Wage growth started to pick up in 2014. With unemployment low, further gains seem likely in 2015: the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IG Metall\f28 \uc2 \f2  union won a 3.4% increase for 800,000 workers in the metals and electrical engineering sector in February. With inflation low, German real wages could rise 2.9%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  thinks -- the highest outcome since 1995. Germany has seen years of wage restraint, while savings now offer scant reward. Perhaps investors ought to get used to the sight of Germans going shopping. (richard.barley@wsj.com; @RichardBarley1) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1628 GMT "Today's data confirms that appreciation pressure on the DKK has been massive during January and most of February following the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's end to its peg and \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's announcement of a bond purchase program," says Scandi economist Tina Mortensen at the U.S. bank. "In terms of nominal GDP, total \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in January and February amounted to around 14% of GDP...The DKK has weakened recently but if it shows renewed strength, the DNB would be forced to resume \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and, likely to cut the certificate of deposit rate deeper into negative territory, in our view." Citi says such action would be well in line with previous comments from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that there is "no limit to how high \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves can go". (Katie.martin@wsj.com; @katie_martin_\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 fx\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:17 EST - Small-business hiring grew for a second month in February as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Paychex\f28 \uc2 \f2 /IHS Small Business Jobs Index rose 0.19% to 100.84. There was a slowdown in the fall and early winter, and the index is down 0.31% from a year earlier. The measure reads same-store, year-over-year worker counts at roughly 350K small businesses nationally with fewer than 50 employees. (ruth.simon@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:02 EST - Desjardins, like CIBC, sticks to its call of another \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  rate cut tomorrow despite the stronger-than-expected 4Q GDP report. The two are contrarians among the primary dealers of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canadian government\f28 \uc2 \f2  securities; the rest changed to no rate change after Poloz's speech last week. Desjardins notes that 75% of 4Q GDP growth was from inventories while domestic demand growth was halved. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:37 EST - PNC thinks Canada's 4Q GDP report hasn't taken the possibility of another rate cut Wednesday from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  off the table. Last quarter's growth was driven by "unsustainably rapid" inventory accumulation--a sign things could slow in early 2015. The bank adds while the recent stabilization in energy prices indicates the BoC will hold rates, the weak details of GDP report "argue for" a cut. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 03, 2015 12:59 ET (17:59 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150303eb330036k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
