\par\par \b \uc2 'Massive'  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 Denmark \highlight\uc2 , Says Citi -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,277 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 17:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1628 GMT "Today's data confirms that appreciation pressure on the DKK has been massive during January and most of February following the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's end to its peg and \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement of a bond purchase program," says Scandi economist Tina Mortensen at the U.S. bank. "In terms of nominal GDP, total \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January and February amounted to around 14% of GDP...The DKK has weakened recently but if it shows renewed strength, the DNB would be forced to resume \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and, likely to cut the certificate of deposit rate deeper into negative territory, in our view." Citi says such action would be well in line with previous comments from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that there is "no limit to how high \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves can go". (Katie.martin@wsj.com; @katie_martin_\b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 11:17 EST - Small-business hiring grew for a second month in February as the \uc2 Paychex\uc2 /IHS Small Business Jobs Index rose 0.19% to 100.84. There was a slowdown in the fall and early winter, and the index is down 0.31% from a year earlier. The measure reads same-store, year-over-year worker counts at roughly 350K small businesses nationally with fewer than 50 employees. (ruth.simon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:02 EST - Desjardins, like CIBC, sticks to its call of another \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  rate cut tomorrow despite the stronger-than-expected 4Q GDP report. The two are contrarians among the primary dealers of \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  securities; the rest changed to no rate change after Poloz's speech last week. Desjardins notes that 75% of 4Q GDP growth was from inventories while domestic demand growth was halved. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 10:37 EST - PNC thinks Canada's 4Q GDP report hasn't taken the possibility of another rate cut Wednesday from the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  off the table. Last quarter's growth was driven by "unsustainably rapid" inventory accumulation--a sign things could slow in early 2015. The bank adds while the recent stabilization in energy prices indicates the BoC will hold rates, the weak details of GDP report "argue for" a cut. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 1524 GMT As the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's staff forecasts will include 2017 projections for the first time, they may give an indication whether government bond purchases end in September 2016 or run for longer, \uc2 UBS\uc2  economists say. "Should the 2017 inflation forecast...show that HICP is still not back to the ECB's target of 'below but close to 2%', it could be interpreted as a first indication that the ECB positions itself toward an extension of quantitative easing beyond September 2016," they say. \uc2 UBS\uc2  reckons the likelihood of QE being extended beyond September 2016 "is not small". (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:11 EST - CIBC World Markets maintains the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  will cut rates for a second consecutive time tomorrow despite stronger-than-expected 4Q GDP. The majority of forecasters have shifted to a pause outlook after BoC Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Stephen Poloz\f28 \uc2 \f2  said last week January's cut buys it time to see how the economy responds. CIBC Chief economist Avery Shenfeld dismisses the 4Q GDP report as "water under the bridge;" he says it wont' have captured coming downdraft from declining prices for oil, a key Canadian export. But he doesn't' hold a strong view on tomorrow's rate outcome. "Our message to investors is, don't have a lot of money riding either way on the timing of a cut." (nirmala.menon@wsj.com, @NirmalaMenon)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:10 EST - CEOs at large US companies see the economy accelerating modestly this year but are holding steady on hiring plans, according the Business Roundtable's 1Q survey. The CEOs expect GDP rising 2.8% this year, versus 2014's 2.4%--which is what the 4Q survey showed was the executives' 2015 forecast. Unchanged, though, is 40% increasing hiring the next 6 months; 23% expect to cut staff. (eric.morath@wsj.com; @ericmorath)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9:59 EST - The era of forward guidance is over and the new word is flexibility, says Torsten Slok, chief international economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 . He believes comments from both Yellen and Fischer the past week signal the shift. But the change may cause anxiety in financial markets and generate volatility. "If the Fed doesn't hold the hand of markets, then markets will come to its own conclusions about the future path of monetary policy." Slok points to 2013's taper tantrum. "When the Fed removes forward guidance, volatility will go up. Bottom line: \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  is telling us that the days of low [volatility] are over." (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1452 GMT 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Bund yields edge 1-2bps higher in relatively quiet trading conditions as traders eye the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meeting on Thursday and the monthly U.S. labor figures on Friday. Gilts yields move 5bps higher after a weaker-than-expected 5-year sale, that saw buyers baulk at the yield on offer, and better-than-expected U.K. construction data. In the 10-year sector, Bunds yield 0.36%, USTs 2.086% and gilts 1.844%. (nick.cawley@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9:46 EST - Capital Economics, veteran bears on Canada's economy, say the annualized 2.4% gain in Canada's GDP in 4Q looks encouraging enough, but it represents the calm before the storm. Now is when the energy-price slump begins to feed through. The "first hints" of the impact of lower oil prices is evident in the breakdown of 4Q growth: engineering investment contracted at a 2% while real gross domestic income, which adjusts for shifts in the terms of trade on GDP, contracted 0.4%. That as 4Q's GDP growth "was driven principally by a pickup in inventory accumulation, which is never an encouraging sign." (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9:45 EST - New York City business conditions rebounded in February, although hiring contracted, according to the latest report from the New York chapter of the ISM. The ISM-NY business conditions index bounced back to 63.1 in February from 44.5 in January. The employment index, however, fell to 49.3 last month. A reading below 50 means a decline in employment. When asked what was the biggest impediment to business, the top answer was bad weather. Bad weather was the second most often obstacle cited in January, a sign that the harsh weather is curtailing business activity in the New York City area. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9:24 EST - While the 2.4% 4Q GDP growth is a pretty impressive result for Canada as many had expected a soggy performance, but \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  isn't giddy. It notes the bulk of the growth can be attributed to inventories and consumption, while to the downside there was considerable weakness in business investment. "Final domestic demand slowed significantly to 1.5%." BNP says the data support its forecast of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  holding steady tomorrow, but it sees the retracing in demand and weak business investment as suggesting a softening in underlying momentum. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 03, 2015 11:28 ET (16:28 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150303eb33002qx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
