\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Crown hits 8-month low as Denmark raises capital controls option\b0\par\par\uc2 466 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2015\par 15:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds fresh quote)\par \par \uc2 By Anirban Nag\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 20 (Reuters) - The Danish crown fell to its lowest in over eight months against the euro on Friday, after a government adviser said the country would be prepared to introduce capital controls to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Hans Jorgen Whitta-Jacobsen, the head of the Economic Council, told Reuters the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was willing to use extreme measures including capital controls to defend the peg to the euro "to the last drop of blood".\par \par \uc2 The crown fell to 7.4640 per euro, its lowest since early June 2014. The euro was last up 0.23 percent at 7.4615 crowns, a substantial move for a pair that usually trades in a 0-0.01 percent range, and on track for its biggest daily move since 1999, when the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was introduced.\par \par \uc2 "The news that capital controls are being considered was the main reason for the lower crown," said a trader at a Danish bank in Copenhagen. "It has been pretty volatile, but it is too soon to say that the upward pressure on the Danish crown is ebbing."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows into Denmark have picked up on speculation it may abandon the peg after Switzerland shocked markets by removing its franc/euro cap on Jan. 15.\par \par \uc2 Since then, the \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and cut interest rates deep into negative territory. Its deposit rate of minus 0.75 percent is the same as the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's.\par \par \uc2 Whitta-Jacobsen said there was no parallel between the Swiss and Danish cases, and two weeks ago \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \uc2 Lars Rohde\uc2  told Reuters the bank would do "whatever it takes" to protect the fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Under the Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM II) set up with the launch of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Denmark agreed to keep the crown in a corridor of 2.25 percent either side of a central rate of 7.46038 to the euro. In practice, it has kept it within 0.50 percent.\par \par \uc2 It spent a record $15 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the tight range.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said worries about Greece and an imminent \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond purchase programme were likely to put further upward pressure on the crown.\par \par \uc2 In response, \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively, cut rates further or possibly launch its own quantitative easing (QE) programme of asset purchases.\par \par \uc2 ING said that, given Denmark's task of sending effective signals to the market had become increasingly difficult, "we believe that QE may eventually be required." (Reporting by Anirban Nag; Editing by Robin Pomeroy and John Stonestreet) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-2-20T15:57:22.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150220eb2k00hjo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
