\par\par \b \uc2 *Denmark  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2 :  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Reserve Rose By DKK106.6B to DKK564.1B in Jan\b0\par\par\uc2 315 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2015\par 16:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 3 Feb 2015 10:03 ET *\b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Net Purchase of \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Market \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  Equal to DKK106.3B in Jan\par \par \uc2 3 Feb 2015 10:29 ET  Denmark Increased Krone Sales in January to Protect Peg\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Charles Duxbury \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Denmark's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Tuesday that it ramped up its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buys in January, selling Danish kroner in the market to weaken it and protect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's peg against a falling euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Nationalbanken, said it bought foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  worth 106.3 billion Danish kroner ($16.34 billion) in January as part of its market inventions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank said its total \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves rose by 106.6 billion kroner to 564.1 billion kroner when market inventions and central government's net borrowing were included.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In December the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  didn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dramatic rise in its market activity reflects Nationalbanken's efforts to counter the upward pressure on the krone generated by investors moving funds to Denmark seeking better returns than they can get in the eurozone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Returns there fell after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced an expansion of a bond buying plan, pushing down the value of the euro and lowering bond yields in the eurozone compared with those available at some durations in Denmark.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nationalbanken's main policy aim is to hold the euro's exchange rate within 2.25% either side of 7.46038 kroner to keep inflation low and provide stability for exporters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As well as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Denmark\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has cut its deposit rate to minus 0.5% and suspended bond issuance to deflect foreign investments away the country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Charles Duxbury at charles.duxbury@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 03, 2015 10:29 ET (15:29 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150203eb2300389\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
