\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Denmark \highlight\uc2  to  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  More To Curb DKK Appreciation -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,323 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2015\par 17:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1637 GMT Today's cut by the \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prompted by the DNB's strong commitment to its exchange rate peg to the euro, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . There were probably strong capital flows into Denmark after the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  scrapped its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floor Jan. 15, compounded by the ECB's quantitative easing announcement last week. More action from the \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to come, Citi's economists say. "As we expect demand for Danish kroner likely will remain high, we reckon the DNB will be forced to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and, likely could be forced to cut the CD-rate deeper into negative territory," strategists said. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 1636 GMT Consumer demand in Russia was for many years the main driver of economic growth. Now things have changed. According to Nielsen, which studies consumers across Europe, the fourth quarter saw the largest drop in consumer confidence in the last three years. In December, an average Russian was behaving like a consumer during the 2008-2009 crisis, the survey found. Confidence has been hammered by a rapid depreciation of the ruble, dropping oil prices and the fallout of Western sanctions, Nielsen says. And there are no positive expectations in sight -- 60% of Russians fear that prices will continue rising further, while 30% expect quality and assortment of goods on stores' stalls to worsen. (andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1629 GMT - OTP Mortgage Bank, part of \f28 \uc2 \f2 OTP Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (OTP.BU) is set for a syndicate issuance in the form of floating rate notes targeting 500 million euros ($565M). The notes are priced 65 basis points above three-month Euribor, which is currently at 0.053%. The maturity of the loan is July 28, 2017 and settlement is the Feb. 5, 2017. The bookrunner is \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (veronika.gulyas@wsj.com; @VeronikaGulyas1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:25 EST - Shares in Mexican grocer Soriana (SORIANA.MX) are up 1.2% after the retailer inked a $2.67B deal to purchase 160 stores from competitor Comercial Mexicana (COMERCI.MX), even though analysts are concerned that SORIANA lacks the capital to make the new assets outperform. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  figures that SORIANA will need to issue more than $1B in equity to reduce leverage, thus diluting minority shareholders. "Soriana faces a tough dilemma," says the investment bank. "If they minimize the portion of this transaction that is funded with equity, then the company might not have the cash to invest in much needed maintenance (and now integration) projects. This could even widen the execution gap vs. competitors." (amy.guthrie@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:07 EST - While the Fed's latest statement continues to suggest a move toward starting rate hike around midyear, BlackRock fixed income CIO Rick Rieder sees increased risks of that not happening amid myriad uncertainties internationally. While saying the dollar's strength shouldn't place the US economic recovery at risk, he notes the greenback is clearly having an impact on export conditions and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  competitiveness while presenting an earnings headwind for companies. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 's "excessively easy policy" has to be moderated despite this "near-term \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  headwind," argues Rieder, as its costs and risks "are simply too great." (daisy.maxey@wsj.com; @DaisyMaxey)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1605 GMT Denmark's third interest rate cut in two weeks probably means borrowing costs will fall to zero for the bond auction next Wednesday. The government plans to launch a new bond due in 2020, with a coupon of 0.25%. It'll likely now be sold at a hefty premium above face value. Denmark doesn't have any other bond maturing in 2020; the one maturing in 2019 is trading currently at a yield of -0.13%, while the one maturing in 2021 is yielding around 0.05%, according to Tradeweb. (emese.bartha@wsj.com @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:03 EST - Scotiabank says its index for Canadian commodity prices has hit its lowest level since the throes of the recession in mid 2009. It says the widespread softness in commodity prices is being driven by a fight for market share in international oil and iron ore markets, as well as general unease over lackluster global economic conditions and an almost "deflationary" environment, particularly in the euro zone and Japan. Looking ahead, Scotia sees oil prices recovering moderately by mid-2015 alongside a global supply adjustment, while a "safe-haven" bid re-emerges for gold, lifting TSX gold equities. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1603 GMT Russia's international reserves shrank by $120 billion last year to $378 billion, its lowest since 2009. At this rate of depletion, the reserves will fall below $100 billion, a minimum level needed to cover short-term external debt payments, by the beginning of 2017, says Capital Economics. "Accordingly, assuming there is no change in the external environment (oil prices, Ukraine conflict, etc.) it seems that Russia has sufficient reserves to maintain current policy settings for about two years." (andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1554 GMT Phyllis Papadavid, senior global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London says that the Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's third interest rate cut in the space of two weeks is "symptomatic of the pressure that is building on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the need for the bank to lean against the wind". "They have ample ability to do so," she says. (josie.cox@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1551 GMT Portuguese airline \f28 \uc2 \f2 TAP SGPS SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  has temporarily banned the sale of tickets in Angola for departures that don't take place in the country because of troubles with the exchange rate. Oil-reliant Angola has seen its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plunge in value as crude prices have slumped. Foreigners have been buying tickets in Angolan kwanza for departures from other countries, but TAP loses out because its costs are in mostly denominated in dollars and euros. Nor would it want to be stranded with plenty of kwanza it can't get out of Angola because of capital controls. A TAP spokesman said a similar ban has been in place in oil-dependent Venezuela since last year. (patricia.kowsmann@wsj.com @kowsmann) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:43 EST - The low level of rains in certain regions of Brazil, which has hurt Sao Paulo state water utility Sabesp, now is expected to impact the water utility in Minas Gerais state Copasa, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 . This week, Minas Gerais governor asked for customers to curb water usage amid drought, which is likely to hurt Copasa's operating revenues and cash flows, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:39 EST - Smallpox vaccine maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Siga Technologies\f28 \uc2 \f2  (SIGA) wants to pay four top executives bonuses for sticking with the company through its Chapter 11 bankruptcy, but it doesn't want the public to know how much they'll get. SIGA is asking a bankruptcy court judge to keep the bonuses under seal because making it public could negatively impact its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and infringe on the privacy of the employees. The four executives earned a total of $4.9M in compensation in 2013, filings show. SIGA, which contributes smallpox vaccine Tecovirimat to the US Strategic National Stockpile, could owe drug maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 PharmAthene\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PIP) $190M in a long-running licensing dispute that led to SIGA's bankruptcy filing. (sara.randazzo@wsj.com; @sara_randazzo)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 29, 2015 11:37 ET (16:37 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150129eb1t0033g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
