\par\par \b \uc2 Danish crown at 2-1/2 yr high vs euro, some bet peg could come under threat\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag   \par \uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2015\par 15:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 19 (Reuters) - The Danish crown hit a 2-1/2 year high against the euro on Monday as investors seeking a safe haven switched from the Swiss franc in the wake of Switzerland's move to abandon its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cap and impose hefty negative interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Traders said some speculators were betting that Denmark could be next to abandon its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the euro, given expectations for prolonged euro weakness if the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opts for quantitative easing. Analysts, however, said they expected the Danish peg to remain intact.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the crown from gaining beyond 7.43 crowns per euro, traders said. There was also talk that it could cut interest rates further into negative territory to rein in the crown.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is buying euro and selling Danish to keep it at these levels," said a senior trader with a Scandinavian bank. "They can continue being here without any problem, but they can also cut the rate or let the euro rate go lower."\par \par \uc2 The ECB is expected to launch QE, possibly announcing such a scheme as soon as at its meeting on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The crown was at its highest against the euro since mid-2012 , when markets were betting Greece would leave the euro. The rise comes just days after the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  scrapped its three-year-old cap on the franc's value against the euro.\par \par \uc2 The SNB also took interest rates deeper into negative territory on Thursday, cutting the benchmark to -0.75 percent, compared with Denmark's -0.05 percent.\par \par \uc2 "With Denmark offering a higher interest rate than Switzerland and lower volatility we could see further capital flow redirected to the Danish crown," said Peter Rosenstreich, head of strategy at \uc2 Swissquote\uc2 , Geneva.\par \par \uc2 Like the franc, the crown is seen as a safe haven in times of economic stress.\par \par \uc2 Denmark is in the \uc2 European Union\uc2  but not the euro zone. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Nationalbank, aims to keep the crown within a band of 7.29252 to 7.62824 per euro. On Monday, it was trading at 7.4350, some distance from the central parity rate of 7.46038.\par \par \uc2 Between September and November 2014, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought 6.9 billion Danish crowns ($1.1 billion) in the market. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is typically its first step before a rate move.\par \par \uc2 ABANDON THE CROWN PEG?\par \par \uc2 Speculation the Nationalbank could give up the peg, especially if the ECB opts for outright money-printing, has gathered momentum. Bearish sentiment towards the euro has also picked up ahead of a Greek election this weekend.\par \par \uc2 The spectre of a lengthy period of euro weakness and years of costly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are seen as behind the SNB's decision to ditch the franc cap.\par \par \uc2 "In the wake of the SNB's decision to drop the floor, markets are already looking for the next 'peg' to go," said Geoffrey Yu, a strategist with Switzerland's \uc2 UBS\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 "Realistically, the crown is the only \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which may have the ability to absorb large amounts of inflows," adding that he did not expect the Danish peg to go.\par \par \uc2 Both \uc2 UBS\uc2  and \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  said there were differences between the Swiss and Danish regimes, pointing to widespread support for the peg in the Danish political system.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the market was not convinced.\par \par \uc2 "After the Swiss action all bets are off," said one Denmark-based trader. (Additional reporting by Partick Graham; Editing by Susan Fenton) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-1-19T15:00:07.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150119eb1j00dde\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
