\par\par \b \uc2 NORDIC MORNING BRIEFING: Eyes on U.S. Earnings, CPI\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katarina Gustafsson   \par \uc2 1,409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2012\par 05:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR-All times GMT \par  \par Nordic Macro \par n/a Swedish PM Reinfeldt meets \par German Chancellor Merkel \par  \par Other Macro \par 0830 UK PPI for Sep \par 0830 UK CPI for Sep \par 0830 Spain t-bill auction \par 0900 Greece t-bill auction \par 0900 EU CPI for Sep \par 0900 Germany ZEW indicator of \par economic sentiment for Oct \par 1000 EFSF auctions up to \par EUR2B 6-month bills \par 1230 US CPI for Sep \par 1315 US industrial production \par and capacity utilization \par for Sep \par 1400 US NAHB housing market \par index for Oct \par  \par Nordic Equities \par 0600 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fabege AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  3Q \par 0900 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Castellum AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  3Q \par  \par Other Equities \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 IBM Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . 3Q \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Coca-Cola Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . 3Q \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  3Q \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Intel Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . 3Q \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Johnson & Johnson\f28 \uc2 \f2  3Q \par  \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors have plenty to look forward to Tuesday as the agenda offers a flurry of earnings from major U.S. companies, a string of data and government debt auctions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. inflation data at 1230 GMT is one of the highlights. Later in the afternoon, U.S. industrial and housing data is also expected.\par \par \uc2 "The recent surge in gasoline prices continued in September, pointing to another big gain in overall U.S. consumer prices," Capital Economics said in a note.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, inflation data is expected too and troubled nations Spain and Greece are both due to hold government debt auctions in the morning.\par \par \uc2 The European earnings season kicks off in earnest this week. The U.S. earnings season started a little earlier and a number of giant corporations release third quarter figures Tuesday as earnings reports are expected from the likes of \uc2 International Business Machines Corp\uc2 . (\uc2 IBM\uc2 ), \uc2 Coca-Cola Co\uc2 . (KO) and \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc\uc2 . (GS).\par \par \uc2 Another highlight this week occurs Thursday when the EU summit will be in the spotlight. As European leaders meet, Spain and Greece will likely be top on the agenda. As for Greece, there is uncertainty over when the debt-laden country will receive its next tranche of aid from its troika of lenders consisting of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And for Spain, investors are keen to see when the troubled nation will request financial aid.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par Latest 0150 GMT  7.3874-972 \par Previous 2150 GMT 7.3917-4003 \par %Chg -0.05 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par Latest 0150 GMT 8.6213-342 \par Previous 2150 GMT 8.6199-297 \par %Chg  +0.03 \par  \par  \par USD/NOK \par Latest 0150 GMT   5.7037-120 \par Previous 2150 GMT  5.7091-144 \par %Chg -0.07 \par  \par  \par USD/SEK \par Latest 0150 GMT  6.6556-646 \par Previous 2150 GMT  6.6578-638 \par %Chg -0.01 \par  \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders will have their eyes fixed on mainly U.S. inflation and industrial production Tuesday, but also Greek and Spanish short term government debt auctions will attract plenty of attention.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lately, U.S. job figures and retail sales have surprised positively, and a continuance of that trend would hold a hand under the Scandinavian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and mainly the export-dependent Swedish krona, said SEB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Richard Falkenhall. still, Mr. Falkenhall said, the upside as well as downside risks to the Nordic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from macro news Tuesday are moderate, because the market has already locked in on the monetary policy meetings of Norway and Sweden's central banks later this month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market sees the potential of a 25 basispoints Swedish rate cut on October 31 at just over 50%, but Falkenhall holds this for unlikely. "We think it's too aggressive. We think they will leave the rate unchanged for now," he said. He estimated the next rate cut won't come until the December policy meeting, although a high level of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  inflows is seen to continue, causing the Swedish krona to gradually appreciate against the euro. SEB has a negative short-term outlook on Norway's krone - mainly due to planned foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buying by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in November - but expects it to appreciate going into the new year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40 1,020.99  +9.89   +1.0% \par OMXS30 1,063.71  +5.01   +0.5% \par OBX      417.76  -0.10  -0.02% \par OMXC20   496.66  +5.25   +1.1% \par OMXH25 2,046.96 +13.67   +0.7% \par  \par Brent  $115.86  +$0.06     0.0% \par  \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This week's most important event will likely be the EU top meeting that takes place Thursday to Friday," market strategist Michael Canborn at IG said in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Market participants want clarity on whether Greece will receive money from EU/IMF and if a rescue package is in the cards for Spain," Mr. Canborn added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Plenty of major U.S. companies report third quarter figures Tuesday while the Swedish earnings season kicks off Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Slumping commodity prices and weak leading indicators point to a tough Q3 reporting season," Handelsbanken Capital Markets said in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "On top, expectations for next year look aggressive, with consensus expecting an overwhelming majority of companies to post higher margins," Handelsbanken added and said healthcare and financials are "the best picks from a bad bunch."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Third quarter figures from companies like investment firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Investor AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  (INVE-B.SK), telecommunication services provider \f28 \uc2 \f2 Teliasonera AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  (TLSN.SK) and ball bearings maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 SKF AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  (SKF-B.SK) are expected Wednesday. Investors are likely to keep an extra close eye on SKF which is viewed as a bellwether of manufacturing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thursday is also a busy day of earnings in the Nordics, with third quarter earnings from troubled Finnish handset maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nokia\f28 \uc2 \f2  (NOK) on the agenda.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude was mixed in Asia on Tuesday with global economic concerns putting a lid on prices which were supported by tighter European Union sanctions against Iran, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for November delivery shed 22 cents to $91.63 a barrel while Brent North Sea crude for November delivery added six cents to $115.86.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude was "holding relatively steady" as fresh Iranian sanctions tussled with global economic worries for traders' attention, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Victor Shum\f28 \uc2 \f2 , senior principal of Purvin and Gertz energy consultants in Singapore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We have concerns about the economy, in particular Europe exerting downward pressure, but supporting oil are the tighter sanctions on Iran from the EU," he told AFP.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European Union foreign ministers on Monday agreed tough sanctions against Iran, aimed at forcing a breakthrough in talks on Tehran's nuclear programme, and also the regime in war-ravaged Syria.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The new sanctions target EU dealings with Iran's banks, shipping and gas imports. Details on those targeted will be released later Tuesday but a government minister is on the new blacklist, diplomats said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, traders were looking to an EU summit commencing Thursday for any breakthroughs on the fragile regional economy as debt-straddled Spain continues to hold off on requesting for a bailout while Greece negotiates for future funding.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEWS ROUNDUP: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hennes & Mauritz AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  (HM-B.SK), the Swedish fashion retailer, Monday said total sales in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose 15% in September compared with a year earlier, a tad higher than previously communicated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norsk Hydro ASA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (NHY.OS), the Norwegian aluminum producer, and consumer goods conglomerate \f28 \uc2 \f2 Orkla ASA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ORK.OS) said they have agreed to join forces, building systems and tubing businesses as they react to toughening conditions and prepare for future emerging market growth creating an equal joint venture called Sapa.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Netflix Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (NFLX), the U.S. online video company, has kicked off its Scandinavian expansion by launching its video streaming service in Sweden.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Clas Ohlson AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  (CLAS-B.SK), the Swedish home improvement retailer, said its sales in September increased 6% from September last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Sweden's finance minister Anders Borg said Saturday that it is likely Greece will end up leaving the euro zone, a move that would establish a separate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that could help the troubled southern European economy regain competitiveness.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 DENMARK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : Earlier this year the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in markets to weaken the krone. It is expected to make another foray soon -- this time to prop up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The Nationalbanken may even go so far as to raise interest rates to protect its precious peg, market participants say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Katarina Gustafsson at katarina.gustafsson@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Flemming Hansen in Copenhagen contributed to this report.) [ 16-10-12 0306GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 77791   \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020121016e8ag00005\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
