\par\par \b \uc2 NORDIC MORNING BRIEFING: European Debt Crisis Remains In Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katarina Gustafsson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2011\par 05:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Energy Service\par \uc2 NRG\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All times GMT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par n/a Helsinki markets closed \par 0730 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yara International ASA\f28 \uc2 \f2  capital markets day in Oslo \par 1000 EU preliminary GDP for 3Q \par 1100 Germany manufacturing orders for October \par      IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The run-up to a key European meeting later in the week will attract attention Tuesday as Europe's debt worries remain in the spotlight. Injecting optimism in the markets, Italy last weekend proposed measures to cut its debt burden -- the second biggest in the euro area -- while French and German leaders Monday signaled their determination to tackle the debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Monday, German Chancellor Angela Merkel said a change to Europe's treaty is required to implement tougher fiscal rules on member nations. The comments came as German and French leaders met ahead of the European summit which will be held in Paris Dec. 8-9.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Furthermore, Italy late Sunday unveiled further budget cuts and growth measures, helping push Italian bond yields down Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Little in major data is scheduled Tuesday and few planned corporate events. In the Nordics, the Helsinki markets are closed due to the Finnish Independence Day.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par          Latest    Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily   %Chg \par          0350 GMT   2150 GMT         High    Low     12/31 \par EUR/NOK 7.7249-312 7.7328-434 -0.13 7.7382   7.7250  -0.71 \par EUR/SEK 9.0556-630 9.0512-617 +0.03 9.0624   9.0542  +0.56 \par USD/NOK 5.7761-804 5.7710-84 +0.06   5.7858   5.7680  -0.83 \par USD/SEK 6.7713-64  6.7550-621 +0.23  6.7803   6.7570  +1.02 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ======\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NOK/SEK 1.1694-1.1702         +0.26 1.1711  1.1659   +1.27 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (as of 1516 GMT Monday)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Danish krone, which is pegged to the euro via the European Union's exchange rate mechanism, has come in focus in recent weeks. The Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Nationalbank, has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, in order to weaken the Danish krone against the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a note, SEB pointed at recent data showing that net purchases of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  due to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in November by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Denmark\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was 10.7 billion Danish kroner ($1.94 billion).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has allowed the EUR/DKK exchange rate to slide through the lower boundary of the channel in place since 2007 around 7.44," SEB said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEB expects the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to lower the lending rate versus the ECB on Thursday, reducing the spread from minus 5 basis points to minus 15 basis points.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Denmark's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  allows the krone to move plus or minus 2.25% from a EUR/DDK pegged central parity of 7.46038.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a recent note, Nordea said it expects the Swedish krona to weaken to around 9.50 to the euro in 2012. However, the bank cautioned that the krona probably will weaken more if the euro-zone crisis deepens further.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     STOCK INDEXES: \par OMXN40   896.63 gain 11.24 up 1.3% \par OMXS30   988.71 gain 14.23 up 1.5% \par OBX      354.74 gain 2.71 up 0.8% \par OMXC20   384.73 gain 1.89 up 0.5% \par OMXH25 2,007.01 gain 32.66 up 1.7% \par Brent   $109.23 gain 0.58  up 0.5% \par  \par     NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investor attention in the Nordics and elsewhere is on a European summit later in the week. And with few planned events on the agenda Tuesday, anticipation ahead of the meeting is likely to continue impact trading said equity strategist Jonas Olavi at Nordea.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy last weekend moved to cut its deficit and hope is that the European leaders will continue to take measures to battle their mounting debts when they meet in Paris Dec. 8-9.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But the market is probably prepared that the meeting perhaps won't deliver on all points," Olavi said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As French and German leaders met Monday, German Chancellor Angela Merkel said a change to Europe's treaty is required to implement tougher fiscal rules on member nations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Sunday, Italian Prime Minister Mario Monti's cabinet approved a law aimed at making sure Italy eliminates its budget deficit by 2013. With the second biggest debt load in the euro area, Italy has unveiled a EUR30 billion three-year plan in tax increases, spending cuts, pension overhauls and growth-boosting measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The agenda Tuesday is thin. German manufacturing orders for October is due at 1100 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We don't expect this to disturb markets that much," Olavi said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     ENERGY: Crude prices were down in Asia Tuesday as the euro retreated against the dollar after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  placed major European countries on negative credit rating watch, analysts said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for January delivery, fell 50 cents to $100.49 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent North Sea crude for delivery in January shed 58 cents to $109.23.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude prices tracked the euro's fall after S&P warned Germany, France and four other triple A eurozone nations that they risked losing their top ratings within 90 days, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Crude oil prices gave up gains on Monday after the euro slipped against the dollar" following the S&P report, said Ker Chung Yang, commodity analyst for Phillip Futures in Singapore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     OTHER NEWS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: Norwegian oil and gas firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Statoil ASA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (STO) said that it will invest NOK3.1 billion ($538 million) in the Visund North field, together with its partners. The field is estimated to contain recoverable reserves 29 million barrels of oil equivalents and consist of mainly oil. Petoro, Total (TOT) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 ConocoPhillips\f28 \uc2 \f2  (COP) are also partners in the license.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: Norway's sovereign-wealth fund, a big shareholder in some U.S. companies, is pushing to make it easier to replace directors at companies including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (WFC) over concerns about financial performance and governance. The board of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2  and five other U.S. companies "need to establish better trust with shareholders," said Anne Kvam, global head of ownership policy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Investment Management, which manages the $550 billion Norwegian Government Pension Fund Global. "Governance is a financial risk if there is a flawed structure."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vestas Wind Systems A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  (VWS.KO), the world's largest wind-turbine maker, said an expiration of the U.S. wind energy production tax credit in 2012 would have dramatic consequences for the U.S. wind power market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: The governor of Denmark's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said that Danish economic growth will remain sluggish until 2013, and that a recovery hinges on resolving the European sovereign debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: Danish government is ready to discuss EU treaty changes, the Danish Prime Minister Helle Thorning-Schmidt was quoted by Danish news agency Ritzau on Monday. "What we can say now is that EU treaty changes are one of the subjects we must consider at the next EU top meeting, and it will also form a dimension of the coming six months, when Denmark holds the EU presidency," said Thorning-Schmidt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINLAND: The economy in Finland expanded in the third quarter, most notably due to stronger private consumption and increasing investment. Finland's gross domestic product was up 2.7% on the year in the third quarter, Statistics Finland said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swedish Automobile NV\f28 \uc2 \f2  (SWAN.AE), the owner of Swedish car maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Saab Automobile\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said it is in talks with Zhejiang Youngman Lotus Automobile Co. and a bank in China about taking an equity interest in Swedish Automobile.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: The chief of Swedish fashion retailer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hennes & Mauritz\f28 \uc2 \f2  (HM-B.SK) Karl-Johan Persson said that while China offers great opportunities, competition there is getting tougher. "It takes a lot of commitment to succeed in China today. And the competition is quickly growing tougher as there are so many players moving there," Persson said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thrane & Thrane A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  (THRAN.KO), the Danish global mobile satellite communications manufacturer, said it has signed a memorandum of understanding with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Inmarsat PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ISAT.LN) for a 78 million Danish kroner ($14.1 million) deal. The agreement is for development and delivery of key components to implement new services on the Inmarsat Broadband Global Area Network, or BGAN.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: Salmon farmer Leroy Hydrotech ASA, a wholly-owned subsidiary of Norwegian company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Leroy Seafood Group ASA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (LSG.OS), said it has detected pancreas disease in salmon at one of its facilities and is considering slaughtering the fish to prevent the virus from spreading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Swedish state-owned utility \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vattenfall AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  (VTF.YY) announced the energy company will stop its plans for a carbon capture and storage demonstration plant in Germany due to lack of political will in the country, abandoning a EUR1.5 billion investment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Katarina Gustafsson, Dow Jones Newswires +46-8-5451-3097; katarina.gustafsson@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Sven Grundberg in Stockholm contributed to this report.) [ 12-05-11 2306ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 77791   \par \par \uc2 Dokument NRG0000020111206e7c60000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
