\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Denmark \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves climb on c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2011\par 16:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to 490.3 bln DKK in Sept\par \par \uc2 * Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in market to soften crown for 14.0 bln\par \par \uc2 * Next Danish rate cut could be near - economists\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quotes)\par \par \uc2 COPENHAGEN, Oct 4 (Reuters) - Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves climbed to a new peak in September as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to curb a bullish Danish crown by selling it for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the bank said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Economists said that the Nationalbank could be on the verge of another reduction of secondary interest rates to soften the crown if its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued in the second half of September after its last rate cut on Sept. 15.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's data only gave numbers for the full month, but no indication of exactly when the bank was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It almost certainly did so ahead of its rate cut.\par \par \uc2 "The big question is whether the Nationalbank has been in the market buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since its last rate reduction on September 15, and whether a new independent rate cut is therefore at the door," Nykredit Markets senior economist Tore Stramer said. "That could well be the case."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose by 14.5 billion crowns in September to a record-high 490.3 billion crowns ($87.4 billion) at the end of the month, the Nationalbank said. That just topped a median forecast of 489.7 billion in a Reuters survey of analysts.\par \par \uc2 The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September to soften the crown by selling 14.0 billion crowns for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it said.\par \par \uc2 The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- buying or selling crowns -- to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a narrow fluctuation band versus the euro in line with its fixed exchange-rate monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The reserves grew by the equivalent of 61.6 billion crowns in the period January to September, with the state's net foreign borrowing contributing 32.1 billion, the bank said.\par \par \uc2 "The contribution to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve from central government borrowing is expected to decrease in November, when redemptions on foreign loans of 32.1 billion crowns are due," it said.\par \par \uc2 STRONG CROWN\par \par \uc2 The crown strengthened in September and has been testing 7.44 to the euro recently, moving from around 7.45 at the end of August.\par \par \uc2 It is trading in the strong part of its allowed band of 7.29252 to 7.62824 to the euro in the ERM II system. In practice the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps an even tighter band.\par \par \uc2 The crown firmed to 7.4422 to the euro by 1454 GMT, against levels around 7.4427 before the bank's announcement.\par \par \uc2 "The Danish crown has strengthened while the European debt crisis has raged," Nordea Markets senior analyst Troels Theill Eriksen said.\par \par \uc2 "Danish as well as foreign investors have found Danish papers and crowns attractive, and the big demand has also strengthened the crown against the euro," Eriksen said.\par \par \uc2 If \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market fails to have the desired effect, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  changes interest rates, as it did on Sept. 15 when it cut its certificates of deposit rate and current account rate by a tenth of a percentage point.\par \par \uc2 The bullishness of the crown has led some analysts to expect the Nationalbank to cut secondary interest rates again soon, possibly this week.\par \par \uc2 The bank usually changes its interest rates in lock-step with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  but can also make unilateral adjustments to steady the crown.\par \par \uc2 The ECB is expected to leave interest rates unchanged at its meeting on Thursday. In that case, the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would leave its main policy rate, the lending rate, at 1.55 percent, but could still cut secondary rates.\par \par \uc2 Nordea's Eriksen said a further independent rate cut by Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Nils Bernstein would likely depend on what ECB President Jean Jean-Claude Trichet says on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "New liquidity measures from Trichet at his last rate meeting can contribute to lowering market" Eriksen said.\par \par \uc2 Nykredit's Stramer said: "In concrete terms, we expect the Nationalbank to reduce the certificates of deposit rate and current account rate by 0.10 percentage point."\par \par \uc2 He added that the timing of a cut would depend on whether the inflow of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the reserves was increasing. ($1 = 5.607 Danish Crowns)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Copenhagen newsroom; editing by Stephen Nisbet)\par \par \uc2 DENMARK-RESERVES/ (UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111007e7a400119\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
