\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Denmark \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves hit new peak after  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2011\par 16:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves climb to record 475.7 bln DKK\par \par \uc2 * C.bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for 11.2 bln DKK to soften crown\par \par \uc2 * Economists say a further rate cut could come soon\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quotes)\par \par \uc2 COPENHAGEN, Sept 2 (Reuters) - Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose by a hefty 18.8 billion crowns in August to a new record 475.7 billion crowns ($91.0 billion) as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold crowns to weaken the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the bank said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The crown has recovered to levels seen before the bank's August rate cut after weakening only temporarily, so the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cut rates again before long.\par \par \uc2 The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in August to soften the crown, selling a net 11.2 billion crowns for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Nationalbank said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The reserves grew by 7.1 billion crowns as a result of net government borrowing abroad, the bank said.\par \par \uc2 On Aug. 25 the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Nationalbank, cut two secondary interest rates by 10 basis points to curb the strength of the crown and said the rate reduction followed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to soften the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bank said then that short euro rates had fallen and the widened spread to equivalent Danish rates tended to strengthen the crown so it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 European Union member but euro zone outsider Denmark's policy of holding the crown steady against the euro means the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shifts rates for the sole purpose of keeping the crown around its central parity of 7.46038 per euro.\par \par \uc2 The bank can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, buying or selling crowns for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost or weaken it. If such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  fail to have the desired effect, it changes interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "The interest rate reduction has had the sought-after effect that the rate spread between short Danish market rates and short market rates in the euro zone has narrowed," Nordea Markets senior analyst Troels Theill Eriksen said in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 "But on the other hand, the immediate weakening of the crown was temporary, and the crown is now roughly at the same level as before the rate reduction," Eriksen said.\par \par \uc2 The crown firmed to 7.4491 to the euro by 1452 GMT from 7.4495 just before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement.\par \par \uc2 "So the (strengthening) pressure on the crown is not over," Eriksen added, "and it is still on the cards that there could be a further rate reduction before long."\par \par \uc2 After the financial crisis, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  intentionally built up its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by taking loans and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the bank probably sees no need to build up the reserves further beyond the current record-high levels.\par \par \uc2 For that reason, the bank would probably \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to a limited extent going forward before it would change rates, Eriksen said.\par \par \uc2 "We believe that they will only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for 5-10 billion before another rate cut if necessary," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Looking at today's figures with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 11 billion, one could think that they had a target of around 10 billion and then cut."\par \par \uc2 The reserves have risen every month this year and last slipped in December. ($1 = 5.228 Danish Crowns)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Copenhagen newsroom; Editing by Ruth Pitchford)\par \par \uc2 DENMARK-RESERVES/  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110905e792000vw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
