\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Denmark Ups Key Rate To 5.5% To Defend  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2008\par 12:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    (Adds detail, analyst comment on euro adoption) \par  \par    By Joel Sherwood \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Denmark's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday raised its key policy rate for the second time this month to prop up the country's struggling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the global financial crisis continues to wreak havoc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Danmarks Nationalbank said it increased its key lending rate and the interest rate for certificates and deposits to 5.5% from 5% "as a result of continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Danish krone."\par \par \uc2 Economists highlight that essentially all \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the exception of the dollar and the yen have been struggling in recent weeks amid heightened risk aversion and global de-leveraging.\par \par \uc2 The Swedish krona and Norwegian krone, free-floating Scandinavian peers of the Danish krone, are both more than 20% weaker against the dollar in the past month.\par \par \uc2 "We've been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently," said Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman Karsten Biltoft. "The way it goes with fixed exchange regimes is first you \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , then you raise rates."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2  has typically moved interest rates in line with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the exchange rate for the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been pegged to the euro since 1999.\par \par \uc2 But Friday's move marks the third time this month and fourth this year that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has broken that pattern, amid intensified financial market upheaval.\par \par \uc2 Danmarks Nationalbank Oct. 7 increased the key rate by 40 basis points, while Oct. 8, it kept rates unchanged despite an emergency 50 basis-point cut earlier that day by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Denmark also pushed up rates May 16 by 10 basis points - its first move independent of the ECB in more than two years.\par \par \uc2 The rate increase in Denmark runs counter to a mounting trend for some other developed economies, where rates are heading downward to soften the impact of the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Alongside the ECB, five other major central banks, including the U.S. Federal Reserve, Bank of England and Swedish Riksbank, took part in the emergency rate cuts earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Thursday, Sweden surprised markets with another half percentage-point rate cut to 3.75%.\par \par \uc2 Although helpful for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , economists say Friday's rate increase will further aggravate an already weakening Danish economy suffering from a falling property market.\par \par \uc2 The move is also likely to bolster calls for Denmark to join the euro area, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "The increase isn't surprising because of the weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it's bad news for the Danish economy," said Niels Roenholt, a Copenhagen-based economist at \uc2 Jyske Bank\uc2 , who added that further rate increases could come if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  situation continues to deteriorate.\par \par \uc2 "This is an example of what can happen when a country stays outside the euro area."\par \par \uc2 Danes narrowly voted against euro adoption in a national referendum in 2000, and public sentiment remains divided.\par \par \uc2 But Prime Minister Anders Fogh Rasmussen, a strong euro proponent, has recently stepped up efforts to sway public opinion in favor of adoption, highlighting disadvantages such as the higher interest rates in Denmark and the country's lack of influence on euro-zone crisis-fighting policies.\par \par \uc2 Rasmussen intends to call another referendum on the issue during the current parliamentary term, which runs through 2011.\par \par \uc2 The U.K., Sweden and Denmark are the only three European Union countries left from before 2004 enlargement that haven't joined the single-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  area.\par \par \uc2 Fifteen countries are currently in the euro zone, and Slovakia is set to join Jan. 1.\par \par \uc2 "I will be surprised if the crisis and rate increases don't add support for adopting the euro," said Jyske's Roenholt.\par \par \uc2 Bank Web site: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.nationalbanken.dk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.nationalbanken.dk}}}\par \par \uc2 -By Joel Sherwood, Dow Jones Newswires, +46 85 45 13 092, joel.sherwood@dowjones.com [ 24-10-08 1045GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081024e4ao000ka\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
