\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Denmark hikes interest rates to defend sliding crown\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Levring   \par \uc2 710 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2008\par 10:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds analysts, background, crown rate)\par \par \uc2 COPENHAGEN, Oct 24 (Reuters) - The Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key lending rate on Friday for the second time this month to support the Danish crown and indicated it was prepared to continue to raise rates to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Euro zone outsider Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it raised its one-week certificates of deposits and lending rates to 5.50 percent from 5.00 percent. The less important discount rate was kept at 4.50 percent.\par \par \uc2 Denmark's crown has steadily weakened over the last two weeks after strengthening in the first few days following the last rate rise on Oct. 7. On Friday, after the rate rise, it was trading at 7.4590 crowns to the euro compared with Thursday's low of 7.4609 crowns. However by 0920 GMT, the crown had weakened again and was trading as low as 7.4632.\par \par \uc2 The crown is pegged to the euro in a 2.25 percent band around a central rate of 7.46038 <EURDKK=> in the ERM-2 grid, but has spent most of the last 9 years since the regime was introduced much closer to its central parity than its trading band actually allows.\par \par \uc2 Danish interest rates are now 175 basis points above the ECB equivalent, compared with 35 bps at the start of October.\par \par \uc2 "This is a textbook \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. We've \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market first before lifting interest rates," \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman Karsten Biltoft told Reuters, without saying how much of its reserves the bank had used. "We'll continue to go by the textbook in the future."\par \par \uc2 Denmark's inclusion in the ERM-2 includes a commitment to unlimited \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  credit between the ECB and the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Danske Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist John Hydeskov said the crown had been under pressure all month, despite being traded around or below its central parity, and that Danske believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had succeeded in keeping the crow strong through support \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Even so, they wanted a rate rise now because they probably don't want to lose too much reserves in the long run," he said.\par \par \uc2 Nordea Chief Economist Anders Matzen said the Danish crown was under pressure as a result of the global credit crisis.\par \par \uc2 "We have seen both the Norwegian and Swedish crown under pressure because investors generally pull out of smaller countries, regardless of whether the have a fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or not. This is the pressure that caused Nationalbanken to raise rates to defend the crown," he said.\par \par \uc2 NO INFLATION TARGET\par \par \uc2 Nationalbanken has no inflation or money supply target but adjusts rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the sole purpose of keeping the crown stable against the euro.\par \par \uc2 "As a result of continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Danish crown, \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Nationalbank increases the lending rate and the rate of interest for certificates and deposits from 5 percent to 5.5 percent with effect as from 24 October 2008," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "This is terrible news for the Danish economy, not least for the housing market," said \uc2 Jyske Bank\uc2  Senior Economist Niels Ronholt.\par \par \uc2 "We had hoped that...Nationalbanken could start getting money in again and cut interest rates. But there is pressure on small \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to defend the fixed-rate policy that we have and must therefore raise rates," he said.\par \par \uc2 Usually, the national bank changes its interest rates the same day and by the same margin as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, it unilaterally raised rates on Oct. 7 and then did not lower them in step with the ECB when it cut rates by 50 basis points on Oct. 8, effectively further increasing the rate spread.\par \par \uc2 Denmark's government last week cut its 2008 growth forecasts and predicted growth next year could be lower than previously expected or even snuffed out completely due to the worsened international financial climate. (Writing by Kim Mclaughlin, Copenhagen Newsroom; editing by David Stamp and Andy Bruce)\par \par \uc2 DENMARK-RATES/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|G|DA|FN|NW|SW|RNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081024e4ao000xi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
