\par\par \b \uc2 EU SUMMIT: Nordic Caution Turns Into Skepticism About EU\b0\par\par\uc2 By William Echikson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,083 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2005\par 18:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRUSSELS (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )--Europe's northern success stories always have been cautious about European unity. Now, though, they're becoming downright skeptical.\par \par \uc2 The skepticism goes far beyond frustration over the bogged-down referendum campaign for a proposed \uc2 European Union\uc2  constitution. They feel marginalized in an expanded bloc of 25 countries and disagree with the direction of the E.U.'s spending on agriculture and poor regions. At the same time, with the euro-zone economy sagging, Sweden, Finland and Denmark are busy lowering their own growth forecasts.\par \par \uc2 "We don't have time for grandiose visions of Europe," Finnish Foreign Minister Erkki Tuomioja told Dow Jones Newswires during a two-day summit of E.U. leaders here. "A pragmatic approach is required."\par \par \uc2 The Nordics traditionally have been at the fringe of E.U. affairs. None entered at the inception of the Union. Denmark joined in 1973, while Sweden and Finland only entered the E.U. in 1995. Norwegians have voted down membership twice and remain outside the E.U. Only Finland uses the euro; Denmark and Sweden have rejected it.\par \par \uc2 And while the three Nordic E.U. member countries have strong welfare states, they are as uncomfortable with French dirigisme as they are with British lassez faire.\par \par \uc2 The most immediate consequence will come with the much-maligned constitution. Opposition to the document, always strong, has soared in recent weeks following French and Dutch No votes.\par \par \uc2 Both Denmark and Sweden said Thursday that they will postpone planned referendums on the constitution. Sweden's Prime Minister Goran Persson said it would be impossible to go to voters if France and the Netherlands refuse to vote anew.\par \par \uc2 Danish Prime Minister Anders Fogh Rasmussen agreed, saying: "We can't put the treaty to a vote in Denmark if there's no treaty to vote on."\par \par \uc2 Even Tuomioja was skeptical about whether his country would go ahead with the much less sensitive parliamentary ratification, scheduled for August.\par \par \uc2 Yet the rest of the E.U.'s leaders at the summit rejected the Nordic request for a full-scale suspension. Instead, they said countries that want to proceed with a vote will be allowed to do so and that the issue will be revisited at another summit in June 2006. That could just postpone tensions: Nordic fervor for a failed constitution looks unlikely to improve in the meantime.\par \par \uc2 "When we see these leaders upstairs behind closed doors debating our future, it makes us uncomfortable," said Jens-Peter Bonde, a leading euro skeptic and Danish member of the European parliament. "The whole thing should be buried." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par        Budget, Enlargement Weigh On Nordic Minds \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another concern is how Europe spends its money. Other E.U. leaders were demanding further cuts to the spending plans for 2007-2013 and lower individual contributions. The Scandinavians, meanwhile, want more money to be spent on research and development and less on agriculture and assistance for poor regions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We want a much more future-oriented budget" devoted "to research and development, innovation and education," Denmark's Rasmussen said Friday. "We should do our utmost to make the E.U. the most competitive economy in the world."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nordic opinion, particularly Sweden's, is key. They want big cuts in their contributions and they would like to see spending in 2007-2013 capped at 1% of the E.U.'s annual gross domestic product.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It's important politically to Sweden's Persson, who has seen support back home melting away, in large part because opposition voices criticize an E.U. budget. "We don't like the old E.U. politics of providing a lot of farm subsidies, etc.," said Lars Lindberg, the Europe spokesman for Sweden's opposition Moderaterna party.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the budget talks break down later Friday, Swedish intransigence could be key. Persson said Friday he prefers "to take a year more than to get a bad decision."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The governments of the Nordic countries, agreed Finland's Tuomioja, "believe they can get a better deal by holding out."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In foreign policy, too, the E.U.'s divisions could have dangerous consequences. Tuomioja worries that his giant neighbor Russia will take advantage of that disarray to "press for advantages" in its negotiations over issues ranging from energy shipments to visa wavers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A more specific foreign-policy concern of the Nordic countries is E.U. enlargement, which they fear will bring in a wave of cheap labor. Swedish labor unions are already blocking the use of Polish and Baltic construction workers on Swedish building projects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nordic countries also fear the impact of the low taxes levied in eastern Europe. They want the E.U. to set minimum levels of VAT and income tax, so that Scandinavia's costly but popular welfare programs won't be put in jeopardy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The E.U.'s present enlargement commissioner, Olli Rehn of Finland, admitted Thursday that the constitutional crisis has generated "enlargement blues," though he insisted "we are not taking any sabbatical from our work." In practical terms, that means Bulgaria and Romania, already accepted, will get in, but other potential applicants such as Croatia, Serbia and above all, Ukraine and Turkey, will have to wait.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Much depends on the economy. Like many others, Danish MEP Bonde believes that the troubled Constitution would have sailed through "if unemployment in France was 5%, not 11%." The Nordics don't face this problem - their economies are robust.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet weakness in the core of Western Europe hurts them, too. The Swedish government recently dropped its annual forecast for growth this year to 3% from 3.2%. In Finland, feeble demand from Europe coupled with a forestry strike has forced the government to pare back growth estimates to 2.5% from 3.0%, and it may have to do so again by next month.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Denmark\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to support the Danish krone's peg with the collapsing euro, bloating its foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. The Norwegians are even more miffed, worried that their rising \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will take their exporters out of the marketplace as their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tightens rates to rein in growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The "economic and political turmoil we now see in continental Europe is slowing us down," Paer Nuder, the Swedish finance minister, recently told Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By William Echikson, Dow Jones Newswires; 32-2-741-1482; william.echikson@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Malin Rising and Louise Nordstrom in Stockholm also contributed to this report.) [ 17-06-05 1647GMT ] \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050617e16h000r0\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspn